BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-113317@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015804Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Founded in 1974\, Uncle Pen Days began as a tribute to Bill Mon
 roe’s maternal uncle\, James Pendleton Vandiver. “Uncle Pen” played the fi
 ddle and Monroe later said that he was the “fellow that I learned to play 
 from.” Bill Monroe’s parents had both died by the time he was 16\, and Mon
 roe lived part of the time this his uncle\, where he would play mandolin w
 hile his uncle played fiddle at local square dances and social events.\nGr
 eatly influenced by Vandiver\, Bill Monroe wrote a song about him\, Uncle 
 Pen\, in 1950 — a song that went on to be a hit for Porter Wagoner (1954) 
 and Ricky Skaggs (1984).
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20230922
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20230923
LOCATION:Bill Monroe Music Park\, 5163 Indiana Highway 135\, Morgantown (in
  Bean Blossom)
RDATE;TZID=America/New_York:20230923T000000
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Uncle Pen Fest
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/uncle-pen-fest/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Founded in 19
 74\, Uncle Pen Days began as a tribute to Bill Monroe’s maternal uncle\, J
 ames Pendleton Vandiver. “Uncle Pen” played the fiddle and Monroe later sa
 id that he was the “fellow that I learned to play from.” Bill Monroe’s par
 ents had both died by the time he was 16\, and Monroe lived part of the ti
 me this his uncle\, where he would play mandolin while his uncle played fi
 ddle at local square dances and social events.</p>\n<p>Greatly influenced 
 by Vandiver\, Bill Monroe wrote a song about him\, Uncle Pen\, in 1950 — a
  song that went on to be a hit for Porter Wagoner (1954) and Ricky Skaggs 
 (1984).</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
