BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-107595@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015804Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:When asked why she killed her infant daughter\, the accused\, a
  young Senegalese-French woman – a PhD student writing on Wittgenstein – a
 nswers\, “I don’t know.  I hope this trial can help me understand.” What w
 ould compel such a shocking act\, and why would an accomplished writer obs
 essively attend the woman’s trial? The complex mysteries at the heart of t
 his absorbing\, wholly original take on both the courtroom drama and the A
 frican immigrant experience\, unfold like a Russian nesting doll of gazes 
 and projections. Is the accused a liar\, a victim\, a sorceress\, or all o
 f the above? In her first fiction film\, Senegalese-French documentarian A
 lice Diop uses her sharp eye for political realism to craft a captivating 
 portrait of motherhood amid cultural isolation.\nin French with subtitles 
 • 122 minutes
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230401T160000
LOCATION:IU Fine Arts Theater (upstairs)\, 1201 East 7th Street\, on the no
 rth side of Showalter Fountain\, next to the IU Auditorium
RDATE;TZID=America/New_York:20230326T160000
RDATE;TZID=America/New_York:20230401T160000
RDATE;TZID=America/New_York:20230402T160000
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Ryder Film Series: SAINT OMER (Two shows\, 4:00 pm and 7:00 pm)
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/ryder-film-series-saint-omer-two-shows-400-pm-an
 d-700-pm/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>When asked wh
 y she killed her infant daughter\, the accused\, a young Senegalese-French
  woman – a PhD student writing on Wittgenstein – answers\, “I don’t know. 
  I hope this trial can help me understand.” What would compel such a shock
 ing act\, and why would an accomplished writer obsessively attend the woma
 n’s trial? The complex mysteries at the heart of this absorbing\, wholly o
 riginal take on both the courtroom drama and the African immigrant experie
 nce\, unfold like a Russian nesting doll of gazes and projections. Is the 
 accused a liar\, a victim\, a sorceress\, or all of the above? In her firs
 t fiction film\, Senegalese-French documentarian Alice Diop uses her sharp
  eye for political realism to craft a captivating portrait of motherhood a
 mid cultural isolation.</p>\n<p>in French with subtitles <strong>• </stron
 g>122 minutes</p>\n<hr />\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
