BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-107679@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015804Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:cicadacinema@gmail.com\; cicadacinema.com
DESCRIPTION:Synopsis: One of the best trans films you’ve likely never heard
  of\, Antonio Giménez-Rico’s landmark 1983 documentary Dressed in Blue (Ve
 stida de Azul) explores the lives and loves of a group of six transgender 
 women living in Madrid in the years following Spain’s transition to democr
 acy. But more than that\, it’s a loving portrait of a culture finally emer
 ging from the shadows after being hidden for far too long. Antonio’s portr
 ait of these women gave them the freedom to portray their own stories with
  their own friends & family\, but in a stylized scripted narrative framewo
 rk with gorgeous cinematography by Teo Escamilla. The stories of Eva\, Nac
 ha\, Loren\, Josette\, Reneé\, & Tamara were never properly released outsi
 de of Spain\, but finally Altered Innocence and Anus Films is proud to pre
 sent this vital testament to just how far we’ve come—and how far there’s s
 till left to go.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230404T190000
LOCATION:The Back Door\, 207 S College Ave\, Bloomington\, IN 47404
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Dressed in Blue (Vestida de Azul) 21+
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/dressed-in-blue-vestida-de-azul-21/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p><span style='
 font-size: small\;'>Synopsis: <b>One of the best trans films you’ve likely
  never heard of\, Antonio Giménez-Rico’s landmark 1983 documentary Dressed
  in Blue (Vestida de Azul) explores the lives and loves of a group of six 
 transgender women living in Madrid in the years following Spain’s transiti
 on to democracy. But more than that\, it’s a loving portrait of a culture 
 finally emerging from the shadows after being hidden for far too long. Ant
 onio’s portrait of these women gave them the freedom to portray their own 
 stories with their own friends & family\, but in a stylized scripted narra
 tive framework with gorgeous cinematography by Teo Escamilla. The stories 
 of Eva\, Nacha\, Loren\, Josette\, Reneé\, & Tamara were never properly re
 leased outside of Spain\, but finally Altered Innocence and Anus Films is 
 proud to present this vital testament to just how far we’ve come—and how f
 ar there’s still left to go.</b></span></p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-107927@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015804Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:When a courageous young woman and a radical lawyer discover a p
 attern of illegal sterilizations in California’s women’s prisons\, they wa
 ge a near-impossible battle against the Department of Corrections. With a 
 growing team of investigators inside prison working with colleagues on the
  outside\, they uncover a series of statewide crimes—from inadequate healt
 h care to sexual assault to coercive sterilizations—primarily targeting wo
 men of color. This shocking legal drama captured over seven years features
  extraordinary access and intimate accounts from currently and formerly in
 carcerated people\, demanding attention to a shameful and ongoing legacy o
 f eugenics and reproductive injustice in the United States. Emmy Award win
 ner for Outstanding Current Affairs Documentary\; 2021 Peabody nominee\; N
 Y Times Critics Pick. [82 min\; documentary\; English]\nA Q&A with Ellen W
 u (Department of History\, Asian American Studies Program\, Center for Res
 earch on Race and Ethnicity in Society)\, Min Joo Lee (Center for Research
  on Race and Ethnicity in Society\, Gender Studies)\, and Jessica Ford (Cr
 eative Industries and Social Sciences\, Newcastle University) will follow 
 the screening.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230404T190000
LOCATION:IU Cinema\, 1213 E. Seventh Street Bloomington\,(on the North side
  of the IU Auditorium\, across from Hoagy)
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Film: Belly of the Beast
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/film-belly-of-the-beast/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>When a courag
 eous young woman and a radical lawyer discover a pattern of illegal steril
 izations in California’s women’s prisons\, they wage a near-impossible bat
 tle against the Department of Corrections. With a growing team of investig
 ators inside prison working with colleagues on the outside\, they uncover 
 a series of statewide crimes—from inadequate health care to sexual assault
  to coercive sterilizations—primarily targeting women of color. This shock
 ing legal drama captured over seven years features extraordinary access an
 d intimate accounts from currently and formerly incarcerated people\, dema
 nding attention to a shameful and ongoing legacy of eugenics and reproduct
 ive injustice in the United States. Emmy Award winner for Outstanding Curr
 ent Affairs Documentary\; 2021 Peabody nominee\; NY Times Critics Pick. [8
 2 min\; documentary\; English]</p>\n<p><i><strong>A Q&A with Ellen Wu (Dep
 artment of History\, Asian American Studies Program\, Center for Research 
 on Race and Ethnicity in Society)\, Min Joo Lee (Center for Research on Ra
 ce and Ethnicity in Society\, Gender Studies)\, and Jessica Ford (Creative
  Industries and Social Sciences\, Newcastle University)</strong></i> <i><s
 trong>will follow the screening.</strong></i></p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
