BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-108073@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015804Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:267-280-7454\; cmcgarvey@penguinrandomhouse.com
DESCRIPTION:Author Alex Mar will be discussing her book about the Paula Coo
 per case (Gary\, IN) and its aftermath at an event at Morgenstern Books on
  April 13. If you are interested in speaking with Alex or seeing a copy of
  the book\, contact cmcgarvey@penguinrandomhouse.com\nAbout SEVENTY TIMES 
 SEVEN\nOn a spring afternoon in 1985 in Gary\, Indiana\, a fifteen-year-ol
 d girl kills an elderly woman in a violent home invasion. When Paula Coope
 r is sentenced to death\, no one decries the impending execution of a tent
 h grader. But the tide begins to shift when the victim’s grandson Bill for
 gives the girl\, against the wishes of his family\, and campaigns to spare
  her life.\nThis tragedy in a midwestern steel town soon reverberates acro
 ss the United States and around the world—reaching as far away as the Vati
 can—as newspapers cover the story on their front pages and millions sign p
 etitions in support of Paula.\nAs Paula waits on death row\, her fate spar
 ks a debate that not only animates legal circles but raises vital question
 s about the value of human life: What are we demanding when we call for ju
 stice? Is forgiveness an act of desperation or of profound bravery?\nMar’s
  work is a feat of narrative reporting\, based on five years of research\,
  hundreds of hours of interviews with over 80 individuals\; thousands of p
 ages of documents\, unpublished letters and emails\, newspaper features an
 d photographs\; and dozens of hours of archival footage.\n 
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230413T183000
LOCATION:Morgenstern's Books\, 849 S. Auto Mall Road\, Bloomington\, IN 474
 01
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Author Talk : Alex Mar’s “Seventy Times Seven”: A True Story of Mer
 cy and Murder
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/author-talk-alex-mars-seventy-times-seven-a-true
 -story-of-mercy-and-murder/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Author Alex M
 ar will be discussing her book about the Paula Cooper case (Gary\, IN) and
  its aftermath at an event at Morgenstern Books on April 13. If you are in
 terested in speaking with Alex or seeing a copy of the book\, contact cmcg
 arvey@penguinrandomhouse.com</p>\n<p style='font-weight: 400\;'><strong><u
 >About SEVENTY TIMES SEVEN</u></strong></p>\n<p style='font-weight: 400\;'
 >On a spring afternoon in 1985 in Gary\, Indiana\, a fifteen-year-old girl
  kills an elderly woman in a violent home invasion. When Paula Cooper is s
 entenced to death\, no one decries the impending execution of a tenth grad
 er. But the tide begins to shift when the victim’s grandson Bill forgives 
 the girl\, against the wishes of his family\, and campaigns to spare her l
 ife.</p>\n<p style='font-weight: 400\;'>This tragedy in a midwestern steel
  town soon reverberates across the United States and around the world—reac
 hing as far away as the Vatican—as newspapers cover the story on their fro
 nt pages and millions sign petitions in support of Paula.</p>\n<p style='f
 ont-weight: 400\;'>As Paula waits on death row\, her fate sparks a debate 
 that not only animates legal circles but raises vital questions about the 
 value of human life: What are we demanding when we call for justice? Is fo
 rgiveness an act of desperation or of profound bravery?</p>\n<p style='fon
 t-weight: 400\;'>Mar’s work is a feat of narrative reporting\, based on fi
 ve years of research\, hundreds of hours of interviews with over 80 indivi
 duals\; thousands of pages of documents\, unpublished letters and emails\,
  newspaper features and photographs\; and dozens of hours of archival foot
 age.</p>\n<p> </p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
