BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-108142@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015804Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:moaaberg@gmail.com
DESCRIPTION:Join the Nourishing Heart Yoga community on Earth Day for a fre
 e prayer flag gathering to share our collective vision for a thriving\, ha
 rmonious\, and peaceful Earth.\nThe day begins at the Nourishing Heart Yog
 a Studio where each participant will design colorful compositions on cotto
 n cloth with fresh and dried flora.  Then roll and press  dye bundles with
  hands\, feet\, and body. While the bundles are steamed to complete the pi
 gment transfer\, Nourishing Heart Yoga founder Matece will lead a contempl
 ative and grounding yoga practice focused on Nourishing Mother Earth.\nDep
 ending on the weather that day\, a walk down to Bryan Park to get feet on 
 the actual earth will take place.  As well as unroll  bundles to reveal th
 e beautiful collaboration between hands and mother nature.  Each participa
 nt makes two prayer flags\, one to keep and one to share as a community of
 fering that will be threaded on a single piece of twine and displayed in t
 he Nourishing Heart Yoga Studio.  While threading the flags onto the twine
 \, each participant will have the opportunity to share their vision/prayer
 /healing wish for the world either silently or aloud.  There will be some 
 quotes/poems/prayers available to choose from or the participant can bring
  your own.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230422T153000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20230422T170000
LOCATION:Nourishing Heart Yoga Studio\, 582 E Hillside Dr. #1\, Bloomington
 \, IN 47401
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Nourish Our Mother: Earth Day Prayer Flag Community Gathering
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/nourish-our-mother-earth-day-prayer-flag-communi
 ty-gathering/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><h5><span style=
 'font-size: small\;'>Join the Nourishing Heart Yoga community on Earth Day
  for a free prayer flag gathering to share our collective vision for a thr
 iving\, harmonious\, and peaceful Earth.</span></h5>\n<p>The day begins at
  the Nourishing Heart Yoga Studio where each participant will design color
 ful compositions on cotton cloth with fresh and dried flora.  Then roll an
 d press  dye bundles with hands\, feet\, and body. While the bundles are s
 teamed to complete the pigment transfer\, Nourishing Heart Yoga founder Ma
 tece will lead a contemplative and grounding yoga practice focused on Nour
 ishing Mother Earth.</p>\n<p>Depending on the weather that day\, a walk do
 wn to Bryan Park to get feet on the actual earth will take place.  As well
  as unroll  bundles to reveal the beautiful collaboration between hands an
 d mother nature.  Each participant makes two prayer flags\, one to keep an
 d one to share as a community offering that will be threaded on a single p
 iece of twine and displayed in the Nourishing Heart Yoga Studio.  While th
 reading the flags onto the twine\, each participant will have the opportun
 ity to share their vision/prayer/healing wish for the world either silentl
 y or aloud.  There will be some quotes/poems/prayers available to choose f
 rom or the participant can bring your own.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-108446@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015804Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:The 1960s were a time of change everywhere\, and that includes 
 Bamako\, the capital city of Mali\, a nation only recently independent fro
 m French colonial rule.  Samba\, a young\, idealistic socialist\, works to
 ward creating a more just nation by day and dances with girlfriend Lara to
  Otis Redding\, and the Supremes by night. Filmmaker Robert Guediguian was
  inspired by the vibrant images\, patterns\, and compositions of famed Mal
 ian photographer\, Malick Sidibé. Lara’s orange silk dress and Samba’s daz
 zling white suit\, shot against the striped backdrop of their dance club a
 nd the black and white checked dance floor they cut up — all suggest that 
 change is coming rapidly\, and that life is for living.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230422T160000
LOCATION:IU Fine Arts Theater (upstairs)\, 1201 East 7th Street\, on the no
 rth side of Showalter Fountain\, next to the IU Auditorium
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Ryder Film Series: DANCING THE TWIST IN BAMAKO (Showings at 4pm and
  7pm)
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/ryder-film-series-dancing-the-twist-in-bamako-sh
 owings-at-4pm-and-7pm/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>The 1960s wer
 e a time of change everywhere\, and that includes Bamako\, the capital cit
 y of Mali\, a nation only recently independent from French colonial rule. 
  Samba\, a young\, idealistic socialist\, works toward creating a more jus
 t nation by day and dances with girlfriend Lara to Otis Redding\, and the 
 Supremes by night. Filmmaker Robert Guediguian was inspired by the vibrant
  images\, patterns\, and compositions of famed Malian photographer\, Malic
 k Sidibé. Lara’s orange silk dress and Samba’s dazzling white suit\, shot 
 against the striped backdrop of their dance club and the black and white c
 hecked dance floor they cut up — all suggest that change is coming rapidly
 \, and that life is for living.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
