BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-108473@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015804Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:http://bloomingtoncontra.org/?fbclid=IwAR1uWvvKt2kiWyTMcXYsna2E9RLt
 G5qwFWDoQW7zWmTxsWHVGmXIeMla6DM
DESCRIPTION:3–5 p.m. English Country Dance\n5–7 p.m. Dinner break (details 
 forthcoming)\n7–10 p.m. Contradance\nAbout the band:\nThe Cosmic Otters ba
 nd features Jonathan Whitall of Chicago on fiddle\, Meg Dedolph of Chicago
  on guitar\, and Eric Schedler of Bloomington on accordion. From their web
 site (https://thecosmicotters.com):\n“We start with a solid rhythmic found
 ation of guitar and piano\, add inventive fiddle harmonies\, and finish wi
 th juicy\, pulsing accordion playing and tasteful percussion.\nThe tunes a
 re from the British Isles and its relatives – Ireland\, England and Scotla
 nd with side trips to Cape Breton and Quebec – but definitely our own\, wi
 th an unshakable drive and playful improvisations that make dancers smile.
  We play for contra dances\, Irish set dances\, English country dances\, a
 nd square dances\, in dance halls\, in houses\, at parties\, at house part
 ies.”\nAbout the caller:\nDan Blim is a caller\, choreographer\, and dance
 r who lives in Columbus\, Ohio\, where he teaches music history at Denison
  University. He loves connecting with the music and connecting with other 
 dancers on the floor\, and bringing a sense of humor. Dan has called and t
 aught English Country Dance and Contra Dance at numerous camps\, including
  Pinewoods in MA\, Camp Cavell in MI and Cascade of Music & Dance in MD. I
 n addition to dancing\, feel free to geek out about musicals\, board games
 \, puns\, Great British Bake Off\, and trivia with him!\nCovid Policies\nU
 p-to-Date Vaccination Requirement: All adult attendees (dancers\, musician
 s\, callers\, observers) are required to have the most up-to-date COVID va
 ccine they are eligible for. Children (under 18) are required to be vaccin
 ated against COVID\, but we are not specifying that they need bivalent boo
 sters at this time.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230429T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20230429T220000
LOCATION:Harmony School Gym\, 909 East 2nd St.\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:A NIGHT OF ENGLISH COUNTRY DANCE AND CONTRA DANCE with Music From T
 he Cosmic Otters and Caller Dan Blim
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/come-join-bloomington-old-time-music-and-dance-g
 roup-with-music-by-the-cosmic-otters-and-calling-by-dan-blim/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>3–5 p.m. Engl
 ish Country Dance<br />\n5–7 p.m. Dinner break (details forthcoming)<br />
 \n7–10 p.m. Contradance</p>\n<p>About the band:<br />\nThe Cosmic Otters b
 and features Jonathan Whitall of Chicago on fiddle\, Meg Dedolph of Chicag
 o on guitar\, and Eric Schedler of Bloomington on accordion. From their we
 bsite (https://thecosmicotters.com):</p>\n<p>“We start with a solid rhythm
 ic foundation of guitar and piano\, add inventive fiddle harmonies\, and f
 inish with juicy\, pulsing accordion playing and tasteful percussion.</p>
 \n<p>The tunes are from the British Isles and its relatives – Ireland\, En
 gland and Scotland with side trips to Cape Breton and Quebec – but definit
 ely our own\, with an unshakable drive and playful improvisations that mak
 e dancers smile. We play for contra dances\, Irish set dances\, English co
 untry dances\, and square dances\, in dance halls\, in houses\, at parties
 \, at house parties.”</p>\n<p>About the caller:<br />\nDan Blim is a calle
 r\, choreographer\, and dancer who lives in Columbus\, Ohio\, where he tea
 ches music history at Denison University. He loves connecting with the mus
 ic and connecting with other dancers on the floor\, and bringing a sense o
 f humor. Dan has called and taught English Country Dance and Contra Dance 
 at numerous camps\, including Pinewoods in MA\, Camp Cavell in MI and Casc
 ade of Music & Dance in MD. In addition to dancing\, feel free to geek out
  about musicals\, board games\, puns\, Great British Bake Off\, and trivia
  with him!</p>\n<p>Covid Policies<br />\nUp-to-Date Vaccination Requiremen
 t: All adult attendees (dancers\, musicians\, callers\, observers) are req
 uired to have the most up-to-date COVID vaccine they are eligible for. Chi
 ldren (under 18) are required to be vaccinated against COVID\, but we are 
 not specifying that they need bivalent boosters at this time. </p>\n</BODY
 ></HTML>
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-108163@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015804Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:http://autismrocksandrolls.com/\; info@autismrocksandrolls.com
DESCRIPTION:On Saturday\, April 29th\, Autism Rocks and Rolls (ARAR) will h
 old its annual Gala celebration at Fountain Square Ballroom in downtown Bl
 oomington\, Indiana.  As part of the proceedings\, ARAR is proud to welcom
 e former Finger Eleven drummer Rich Beddoe to speak about his experiences 
 with autism.\n“Our nonprofit has grown tremendously in the last year.  At 
 this year’s gala\, we are taking our mission of acceptance and adding an e
 lement of inspiration to it because we have met many amazing people along 
 the way that have encouraged and supported our work. As a mother of someon
 e on the spectrum\, I want Sam to be part of a movement of change: a chang
 e that moves toward inclusion\, kindness\, acceptance\, education\, and in
 spiration about differently-abled people\,” Autism Rocks and Rolls Gina Mi
 tchell commented.\nSam Mitchell is the driving force behind Autism Rocks a
 nd Rolls.  As a teen\, Mitchell developed a podcast to connect people with
  autism and remove the stigma that is sometimes attached to the clinical d
 iagnosis. What started as a podcast\, seventy dollars in a bank account\, 
 and a hope to raise awareness has become a full-fledged non-profit that is
  governed by a seven-member board that works with Sam and Gina Mitchell to
  put a community element into autism acceptance.\n“Autism Rocks and Rolls 
 works tirelessly in southern Indiana and throughout the state to raise awa
 reness\, increase communication\, and seek out new ways for organizations\
 , families\, and individuals to connect with each other to share their exp
 eriences about living with autism and the gala is just one manifestation o
 f that.  We hope that the gala can be a conduit for developing more suppor
 t for all people living with and supporting autism. This is also one of th
 e main ways our organization cultivates financial support\,” stated ARAR B
 oard President Steven Jascewsky.\nAs the main fundraiser for Autism Rocks 
 and Rolls\, the gala is in its second year. In addition to speaker Rich Be
 ddoe\, there will be advocacy groups that work with autism in south centra
 l Indiana\, a silent auction\, and food and drink for all attendees.  Whil
 e a 15 dollar donation is encouraged\, everyone will be welcomed and encou
 raged to support Autism Rocks and Rolls in any way possible.\n“The gala wi
 ll bring together people to celebrate our diversity. Sam and ARAR want to 
 help people understand that there is no normal. We also want to help make 
 people realize that there are resources out there to support all people th
 at live with autism. This support can be connecting a family with an organ
 ization or just providing a supportive voice to the daily struggles everyo
 ne faces\,” mentioned Mitchell.\nThe public is encouraged to attend ARAR’s
  second annual Gala celebration on Saturday\, April 29th at Fountain Squar
 e Ballroom in Bloomington beginning at 3 PM.  If you have any questions ab
 out the gala\, ARAR\, or their advocacy and media work\, more information 
 is available online at AUTISMROCKSANDROLLS.COM.  If you are interested in 
 sponsoring the event\, please contact Autism Rocks and Rolls Gina Mitchell
  at 812-699-7811 or via email at info@autismrocksandrolls.com.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230429T150000
LOCATION:Fountain Square Mall Ballroom\, 101 West Kirkwood Avenue\, Bloomin
 gton\, IN 47404
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Autism Rock and Rolls Advocates Inspiration with Second Gala
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/autism-rock-and-rolls-advocates-inspiration-with
 -second-gala/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>On Saturday\,
  April 29th\, Autism Rocks and Rolls (ARAR) will hold its annual Gala cele
 bration at Fountain Square Ballroom in downtown Bloomington\, Indiana.  As
  part of the proceedings\, ARAR is proud to welcome former Finger Eleven d
 rummer Rich Beddoe to speak about his experiences with autism.</p>\n<p>“Ou
 r nonprofit has grown tremendously in the last year.  At this year’s gala\
 , we are taking our mission of acceptance and adding an element of inspira
 tion to it because we have met many amazing people along the way that have
  encouraged and supported our work. As a mother of someone on the spectrum
 \, I want Sam to be part of a movement of change: a change that moves towa
 rd inclusion\, kindness\, acceptance\, education\, and inspiration about d
 ifferently-abled people\,” Autism Rocks and Rolls Gina Mitchell commented.
 </p>\n<p>Sam Mitchell is the driving force behind Autism Rocks and Rolls. 
  As a teen\, Mitchell developed a podcast to connect people with autism an
 d remove the stigma that is sometimes attached to the clinical diagnosis. 
 What started as a podcast\, seventy dollars in a bank account\, and a hope
  to raise awareness has become a full-fledged non-profit that is governed 
 by a seven-member board that works with Sam and Gina Mitchell to put a com
 munity element into autism acceptance.</p>\n<p>“Autism Rocks and Rolls wor
 ks tirelessly in southern Indiana and throughout the state to raise awaren
 ess\, increase communication\, and seek out new ways for organizations\, f
 amilies\, and individuals to connect with each other to share their experi
 ences about living with autism and the gala is just one manifestation of t
 hat.  We hope that the gala can be a conduit for developing more support f
 or all people living with and supporting autism. This is also one of the m
 ain ways our organization cultivates financial support\,” stated ARAR Boar
 d President Steven Jascewsky.</p>\n<p>As the main fundraiser for Autism Ro
 cks and Rolls\, the gala is in its second year. In addition to speaker Ric
 h Beddoe\, there will be advocacy groups that work with autism in south ce
 ntral Indiana\, a silent auction\, and food and drink for all attendees.  
 While a 15 dollar donation is encouraged\, everyone will be welcomed and e
 ncouraged to support Autism Rocks and Rolls in any way possible.</p>\n<p>“
 The gala will bring together people to celebrate our diversity. Sam and AR
 AR want to help people understand that there is no normal. We also want to
  help make people realize that there are resources out there to support al
 l people that live with autism. This support can be connecting a family wi
 th an organization or just providing a supportive voice to the daily strug
 gles everyone faces\,” mentioned Mitchell.</p>\n<p>The public is encourage
 d to attend ARAR’s second annual Gala celebration on Saturday\, April 29th
  at Fountain Square Ballroom in Bloomington beginning at 3 PM.  If you hav
 e any questions about the gala\, ARAR\, or their advocacy and media work\,
  more information is available online at <a href='http://autismrocksandrol
 ls.com/' target='_blank' rel='noopener' data-saferedirecturl='https://www.
 google.com/url?q=http://AUTISMROCKSANDROLLS.COM&source=gmail&ust=168124229
 9192000&usg=AOvVaw1UB4sxAXulx-yit3R3rx7n'>AUTISMROCKSANDROLLS.COM</a>.  If
  you are interested in sponsoring the event\, please contact Autism Rocks 
 and Rolls Gina Mitchell at 812-699-7811 or via email at <a href='mailto:in
 fo@autismrocksandrolls.com' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>info@autismrock
 sandrolls.com</a>.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-108656@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015804Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:The 1960s were a time of change everywhere\, and that includes 
 Bamako\, the capital city of Mali\, a nation only recently independent fro
 m French colonial rule.  Samba\, a young\, idealistic socialist\, works to
 ward creating a more just nation by day and dances with girlfriend Lara to
  Otis Redding\, and the Supremes by night. Filmmaker Robert Guediguian was
  inspired by the vibrant images\, patterns\, and compositions of famed Mal
 ian photographer\, Malick Sidibé. Lara’s orange silk dress and Samba’s daz
 zling white suit\, shot against the striped backdrop of their dance club a
 nd the black and white checked dance floor they cut up — all suggest that 
 change is coming rapidly\, and that life is for living.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230429T160000
LOCATION:IU Fine Arts Theater (upstairs)\, 1201 East 7th Street\, on the no
 rth side of Showalter Fountain\, next to the IU Auditorium
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Ryder Film Series: DANCING THE TWIST IN BAMAKO (showings at 4pm and
  7pm)
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/ryder-film-series-dancing-the-twist-in-bamako-sh
 owings-at-4pm-and-7pm-2/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY>\n<p>The 1960s w
 ere a time of change everywhere\, and that includes Bamako\, the capital c
 ity of Mali\, a nation only recently independent from French colonial rule
 .  Samba\, a young\, idealistic socialist\, works toward creating a more j
 ust nation by day and dances with girlfriend Lara to Otis Redding\, and th
 e Supremes by night. Filmmaker Robert Guediguian was inspired by the vibra
 nt images\, patterns\, and compositions of famed Malian photographer\, Mal
 ick Sidibé. Lara’s orange silk dress and Samba’s dazzling white suit\, sho
 t against the striped backdrop of their dance club and the black and white
  checked dance floor they cut up — all suggest that change is coming rapid
 ly\, and that life is for living.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
