BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-109014@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015804Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Tomberlin is Sarah Beth Tomberlin\, a pastor’s kid born in Flor
 ida\, raised in rural Illinois. She wrote the majority of her debut\, At W
 eddings (2018)\, while living at home. For a while after leaving home and 
 church\, she lived in Louisville\, Kentucky. She worked a day job and kept
  writing songs. She posted some of these songs to Bandcamp\, which led to 
 her signing a record deal with Saddle Creek. It all happened fast: Less th
 an a year after her first live show\, she performed on Jimmy Kimmel and sh
 e ended up moving to L.A. which is where she wrote Projections (2020)\, he
 r EP followup to At Weddings.\nDuring the pandemic\, Sarah Beth was all ov
 er the place\, physically and mentally. Louisville. Los Angeles. Back home
  in Illinois for a bit. Brooklyn\, where she’s now settled\, she says. Bro
 oklyn is also where her new album i don’t know who needs to hear this… was
  recorded\, at Figure 8 studios over the course of two weeks\, with produc
 er and engineer Phil Weinrobe (who played a variety of instruments on the 
 collection)\, and later mastered by Josh Bonati\, also in Brooklyn.\n“The 
 theme of the record\,” she explains\, “is to examine\, hold space\, make a
 n altar for the feelings.” Hold space: Tomberlin’s songs do it literally\,
  making it heard space. Her full-length debut\, At Weddings\, was widely p
 raised for the sparsity and delicacy of its instrumentation\, especially i
 n contrast with the emotional heft of her lyrics.\nHere\, the space feels 
 larger and holier\, built to echo. Pedal steel. Old acoustic guitars\, fre
 shly plucked. A drifting synthesizer. Chill\, brushy percussion. Ambient\,
  expansive clarinet and saxophone. Aleatory piano trills\, a lot of piddli
 ng with the occasional splash. The looseness and wideness of the arrangeme
 nts conveys a tender regard for their parts\, as though each arpeggio\, lo
 op\, scratch is a found shell or feather in the hand. Then there is the in
 strument of her voice\, which has the endearing quality of being perfectly
  tuned but reluctantly played. “I’m not a singer\,” she sings on “idkwntht
 .” “I’m just someone who’s guilty.”
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230512T210000
LOCATION:The Bishop Bar\, 123 S Walnut St\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Tomberlin with Trace Mountains
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/tomberlin-with-trace-mountains/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Tomberlin is 
 Sarah Beth Tomberlin\, a pastor’s kid born in Florida\, raised in rural Il
 linois. She wrote the majority of her debut\, At Weddings (2018)\, while l
 iving at home. For a while after leaving home and church\, she lived in Lo
 uisville\, Kentucky. She worked a day job and kept writing songs. She post
 ed some of these songs to Bandcamp\, which led to her signing a record dea
 l with Saddle Creek. It all happened fast: Less than a year after her firs
 t live show\, she performed on Jimmy Kimmel and she ended up moving to L.A
 . which is where she wrote Projections (2020)\, her EP followup to At Wedd
 ings.</p>\n<p>During the pandemic\, Sarah Beth was all over the place\, ph
 ysically and mentally. Louisville. Los Angeles. Back home in Illinois for 
 a bit. Brooklyn\, where she’s now settled\, she says. Brooklyn is also whe
 re her new album i don’t know who needs to hear this… was recorded\, at Fi
 gure 8 studios over the course of two weeks\, with producer and engineer P
 hil Weinrobe (who played a variety of instruments on the collection)\, and
  later mastered by Josh Bonati\, also in Brooklyn.</p>\n<p>“The theme of t
 he record\,” she explains\, “is to examine\, hold space\, make an altar fo
 r the feelings.” Hold space: Tomberlin’s songs do it literally\, making it
  heard space. Her full-length debut\, At Weddings\, was widely praised for
  the sparsity and delicacy of its instrumentation\, especially in contrast
  with the emotional heft of her lyrics.</p>\n<p>Here\, the space feels lar
 ger and holier\, built to echo. Pedal steel. Old acoustic guitars\, freshl
 y plucked. A drifting synthesizer. Chill\, brushy percussion. Ambient\, ex
 pansive clarinet and saxophone. Aleatory piano trills\, a lot of piddling 
 with the occasional splash. The looseness and wideness of the arrangements
  conveys a tender regard for their parts\, as though each arpeggio\, loop\
 , scratch is a found shell or feather in the hand. Then there is the instr
 ument of her voice\, which has the endearing quality of being perfectly tu
 ned but reluctantly played. “I’m not a singer\,” she sings on “idkwntht.” 
 “I’m just someone who’s guilty.”</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-109091@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015804Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:PRENativePlants@gmail.com
DESCRIPTION:Expect milkweed\, asters\, obedient plant\, ox-eye sunflower\, 
 cup plants\, indian physic\, coreopsis\, aronia bushes\, witch hazel\, pec
 an trees\, and more! All plants must be for trade\; no sales permitted. No
 n-invasive cultivars are welcome\, but invasive plants are prohibited. To 
 ensure invasive species and pests are not inadvertently spread\, please po
 t plants you bring in commercial potting soil.\nIf you don’t have plants t
 o share\, then come learn about our Indiana native plants.\nNative plant s
 pecies are ones that evolve naturally in a given region. Wildflowers\, fer
 ns\, mosses\, shrubs\, and trees of our area have adapted to local environ
 mental conditions over thousands of years and form a well-balanced ecosyst
 em.\nNorth American natives are disappearing at an alarming rate due to la
 nd development\, expanding agriculture\, and introduction of invasive spec
 ies. In our own yards\, we can help to sustain natives that provide a food
  and habitat for birds\, butterflies\, beneficial insects\, and small wild
 life.\nThis casual\, come-and-go event is family-friendly. The park has a 
 playground.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230513T080000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20230513T140000
LOCATION:Park Ridge East Park Shelter\, 4421 E. Morningside Drive Bloomingt
 on IN
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Native Plants and Seed Exchange
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/native-plants-and-seed-exchange-2/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Expect milkwe
 ed\, asters\, obedient plant\, ox-eye sunflower\, cup plants\, indian phys
 ic\, coreopsis\, aronia bushes\, witch hazel\, pecan trees\, and more! All
  plants must be for trade\; no sales permitted. Non-invasive cultivars are
  welcome\, but invasive plants are prohibited. To ensure invasive species 
 and pests are not inadvertently spread\, please pot plants you bring in co
 mmercial potting soil.<br />\nIf you don’t have plants to share\, then com
 e learn about our Indiana native plants.<br />\nNative plant species are o
 nes that evolve naturally in a given region. Wildflowers\, ferns\, mosses\
 , shrubs\, and trees of our area have adapted to local environmental condi
 tions over thousands of years and form a well-balanced ecosystem.<br />\nN
 orth American natives are disappearing at an alarming rate due to land dev
 elopment\, expanding agriculture\, and introduction of invasive species. I
 n our own yards\, we can help to sustain natives that provide a food and h
 abitat for birds\, butterflies\, beneficial insects\, and small wildlife.<
 br />\nThis casual\, come-and-go event is family-friendly. The park has a 
 playground.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
