BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-109216@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015805Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Star running back\, Anthony Teague is just a winning season awa
 y from making the big time – but the pressures of being a student athlete 
 threaten to crowd out a promising future. On The Line: A College Football 
 Play\, explores both the humanity and the inanity around universities’ mos
 t visible product\, college football. Set against the backdrop of adoring 
 fans and the money that flows through the sport\, this play explores the m
 achine that is college football and how it enmeshes Anthony and those arou
 nd him.\nOn The Line: A College Football Play was written by Indiana Unive
 rsity Department of Theatre\, Drama\, and Contemporary Dance faculty membe
 rs Jonathan Michaelsen and Ansley Valentine and has been in development si
 nce late 2021. Michaelsen\, whose academic career has included stops at un
 iversities in both Conference USA and the SEC before joining the faculty a
 t IU\, has long observed the contradictions exhibited by academic institut
 ions in their efforts to both encourage and corral the ever-growing influe
 nce of sports\, in particular football\, on college campuses\; “When I was
  on faculty at the University of Alabama\, you couldn’t get close to campu
 s\, much less to your office on game days\; the university was entirely gi
 ven over to the over 100\,000 fans who descended on Tuscaloosa every home 
 game weekend. And I often wondered: does college football exist to support
  the academic mission\, or is it the other way around? I couldn’t resist e
 xploring that question in a play. There is a fair amount of theatricality 
 on display in college football\, and the sport doesn’t lack for colorful c
 haracters\, so the worlds mesh well together”. Valentine adds\, “It is exc
 iting to bring this story to the stage. So many people watch college footb
 all\, but have no idea about the academic supports behind the game. I thin
 k the audience will be fascinated and moved.”
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230518T193000
LOCATION:Ted Jones Playhouse\, 107 W 9th St\, Bloomington
RDATE;TZID=America/New_York:20230519T193000
RDATE;TZID=America/New_York:20230520T193000
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:On the Line: A College Football Play
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/on-the-line-a-college-football-play/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Star running 
 back\, Anthony Teague is just a winning season away from making the big ti
 me – but the pressures of being a student athlete threaten to crowd out a 
 promising future. On The Line: A College Football Play\, explores both the
  humanity and the inanity around universities’ most visible product\, coll
 ege football. Set against the backdrop of adoring fans and the money that 
 flows through the sport\, this play explores the machine that is college f
 ootball and how it enmeshes Anthony and those around him.</p>\n<p>On The L
 ine: A College Football Play was written by Indiana University Department 
 of Theatre\, Drama\, and Contemporary Dance faculty members Jonathan Micha
 elsen and Ansley Valentine and has been in development since late 2021. Mi
 chaelsen\, whose academic career has included stops at universities in bot
 h Conference USA and the SEC before joining the faculty at IU\, has long o
 bserved the contradictions exhibited by academic institutions in their eff
 orts to both encourage and corral the ever-growing influence of sports\, i
 n particular football\, on college campuses\; “When I was on faculty at th
 e University of Alabama\, you couldn’t get close to campus\, much less to 
 your office on game days\; the university was entirely given over to the o
 ver 100\,000 fans who descended on Tuscaloosa every home game weekend. And
  I often wondered: does college football exist to support the academic mis
 sion\, or is it the other way around? I couldn’t resist exploring that que
 stion in a play. There is a fair amount of theatricality on display in col
 lege football\, and the sport doesn’t lack for colorful characters\, so th
 e worlds mesh well together”. Valentine adds\, “It is exciting to bring th
 is story to the stage. So many people watch college football\, but have no
  idea about the academic supports behind the game. I think the audience wi
 ll be fascinated and moved.”</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
