BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-109496@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015805Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Registration is open/Please register Join this book club to dis
 cuss nature-focused reads each month. You’ll also learn how to become more
  ecologically-minded through activities and speakers designed to enhance o
 ur reading. This month\, join us for a tree identification lesson at Winsl
 ow Woods Park (2301 S. Highland Ave.) with retired tree farmer Ray Major\,
  who specialized in growing trees from seed. We will be discussing The Ove
 rstory by Richard Powers. You either love this book\, or it’s been on your
  TBR list since it won the Pulitzer prize for fiction in 2019. We’ll bring
  the blankets\, and you bring any chairs or sun protection you might need!
 \nSummary:\nTold in six interconnecting stories\, Powers draws readers int
 o the quiet\, dynamic\, intelligent lives of the trees that we live around
  and beneath. From poetic descriptions of the biological processes trees g
 o through\, to the narrative arcs of each of the characters\, The Overstor
 y is a novel you won’t forget. Please join us for some invigorating conver
 sations as we wake back up from our winter hibernation.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230525T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20230525T200000
LOCATION:Winslow Woods Park\, 2301 South Highland Ave.\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Green Peeps Book Club: The Overstory (also a tree identification le
 sson with retired tree farmer Ray Major\, who specialized in growing trees
  from seed) Age 18 and up
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/green-peeps-book-club-the-overstory-also-a-tree-
 identification-lesson-with-retired-tree-farmer-ray-major-who-specialized-i
 n-growing-trees-from-seed-age-18-and-up/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p><strong>Regis
 tration is open/Please register</strong> Join this book club to discuss na
 ture-focused reads each month. You’ll also learn how to become more ecolog
 ically-minded through activities and speakers designed to enhance our read
 ing. This month\, join us for a tree identification lesson at Winslow Wood
 s Park (2301 S. Highland Ave.) with retired tree farmer Ray Major\, who sp
 ecialized in growing trees from seed. We will be discussing The Overstory 
 by Richard Powers. You either love this book\, or it’s been on your TBR li
 st since it won the Pulitzer prize for fiction in 2019. We’ll bring the bl
 ankets\, and you bring any chairs or sun protection you might need!</p>\n<
 p>Summary:<br />\nTold in six interconnecting stories\, Powers draws reade
 rs into the quiet\, dynamic\, intelligent lives of the trees that we live 
 around and beneath. From poetic descriptions of the biological processes t
 rees go through\, to the narrative arcs of each of the characters\, The Ov
 erstory is a novel you won’t forget. Please join us for some invigorating 
 conversations as we wake back up from our winter hibernation.</p>\n</BODY>
 </HTML>
END:VEVENT
