BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-109773@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015805Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:https://igws.indiana.edu/events
DESCRIPTION:How do you carve limestone? Where are the best places to hike i
 n the stone belt? How do caves form?\nWhy are so many things built with li
 mestone\, and how important is that industry to this region?\nIf you have 
 any desire to know more about Indiana’s official state stone than you alre
 ady think you do\,\nlocal experts will fill you in at the Limestone Fest S
 aturday\, June 17\, from 10 a.m. to 3 p.m. Hosted by\nthe Indiana Geologic
 al and Water Survey (IGWS)\, this free\, family-friendly event is bringing
  together\nIGWS geologists and representatives from other science- and out
 door-related entities to celebrate\nLimestone Month in Indiana.\nActivitie
 s will be set up mostly behind the IGWS office (1001 E. 10 th St.) in the 
 grassy Northwest Quad on\nCottage Grove Avenue. Free parking is available 
 in the neighboring Forrest Avenue garage.\nThe IGWS’s Learning Lab\, celeb
 rating its one-year anniversary in June\, also will be open on the first f
 loor\nof the IGWS/Geological Sciences building. There\, visitors can pull 
 open drawers and handle thousands of\nrock\, mineral\, and fossil specimen
 s that had been mostly inaccessible to the public before the Learning\nLab
  was created.\nMore information: https://igws.indiana.edu/events
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20230617T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20230617T150000
LOCATION:Indiana Geological & Water Survey\, 1001 E 10th St\, Bloomington\,
  IN 47405
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Limestone Fest
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/limestone-fest/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>How do you ca
 rve limestone? Where are the best places to hike in the stone belt? How do
  caves form?<br />\nWhy are so many things built with limestone\, and how 
 important is that industry to this region?<br />\nIf you have any desire t
 o know more about Indiana’s official state stone than you already think yo
 u do\,<br />\nlocal experts will fill you in at the Limestone Fest Saturda
 y\, June 17\, from 10 a.m. to 3 p.m. Hosted by<br />\nthe Indiana Geologic
 al and Water Survey (IGWS)\, this free\, family-friendly event is bringing
  together<br />\nIGWS geologists and representatives from other science- a
 nd outdoor-related entities to celebrate<br />\nLimestone Month in Indiana
 .<br />\nActivities will be set up mostly behind the IGWS office (1001 E. 
 10 th St.) in the grassy Northwest Quad on<br />\nCottage Grove Avenue. Fr
 ee parking is available in the neighboring Forrest Avenue garage.<br />\nT
 he IGWS’s Learning Lab\, celebrating its one-year anniversary in June\, al
 so will be open on the first floor<br />\nof the IGWS/Geological Sciences 
 building. There\, visitors can pull open drawers and handle thousands of<b
 r />\nrock\, mineral\, and fossil specimens that had been mostly inaccessi
 ble to the public before the Learning<br />\nLab was created.<br />\nMore 
 information: https://igws.indiana.edu/events</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
