 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>PALS(People &
  Animal Learning Services) will have horses in costume\, prizes\, pumpkin 
 decorating\, face painting\, and trick-or-treating on their new Sensory Tr
 ail! Enjoy a cup of coffee from Cup of Joy or dinner at Uno Mas or Big D’s
  BBQ food trucks. Costumes are welcome but optional!</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-114446@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015805Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:A Compassionate Spy is a gripping real-life spy thriller about 
 Manhattan Project physicist Ted Hall\, who provided nuclear secrets to the
  Soviet Union\, told through the perspective of his loving wife Joan\, who
  protected his secret for decades. Recruited in 1944 as an 18-year-old Har
 vard undergraduate to help Robert Oppenheimer and his team create a bomb\,
  Hall was the youngest physicist on the Manhattan Project\, and didn’t sha
 re his colleagues’ elation after the successful detonation of the world’s 
 first atomic bomb. Concerned that a U.S. post-war monopoly on such a power
 ful weapon could lead to nuclear catastrophe\, Hall began passing key info
 rmation about the bomb’s construction to the Soviet Union. After the war\,
  he met\, fell in love with\, and married Joan\, a fellow student with who
 m he shared a passion for classical music and socialist causes — and the e
 xplosive secret of his espionage.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20231028T160000
LOCATION:IU Radio & Television Theater (RTV 251)\, 1229 East 7th St\, Bloom
 ington
RDATE;TZID=America/New_York:20231029T160000
RDATE;TZID=America/New_York:20231104T160000
RDATE;TZID=America/New_York:20231105T160000
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Ryder Film Series: A COMPASSIONATE SPY (screenings at 4pm and 7pm)
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/ryder-film-series-a-compassionate-spy-screenings
 -at-4pm-and-7pm/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>A Compassiona
 te Spy is a gripping real-life spy thriller about Manhattan Project physic
 ist Ted Hall\, who provided nuclear secrets to the Soviet Union\, told thr
 ough the perspective of his loving wife Joan\, who protected his secret fo
 r decades. Recruited in 1944 as an 18-year-old Harvard undergraduate to he
 lp Robert Oppenheimer and his team create a bomb\, Hall was the youngest p
 hysicist on the Manhattan Project\, and didn’t share his colleagues’ elati
 on after the successful detonation of the world’s first atomic bomb. Conce
 rned that a U.S. post-war monopoly on such a powerful weapon could lead to
  nuclear catastrophe\, Hall began passing key information about the bomb’s
  construction to the Soviet Union. After the war\, he met\, fell in love w
 ith\, and married Joan\, a fellow student with whom he shared a passion fo
 r classical music and socialist causes — and the explosive secret of his e
 spionage. </p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-114500@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015805Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Former bandmates and Bloomingtonians John Strohm and Freda Love
  Smith reunite for a special event to celebrate both the release of John’s
  new solo album\, SOMETHING TO LOOK FORWARD TO\, and Freda’s new memoir\, 
 I QUIT EVERYTHING. John and Freda will be joined by WFIU’s David Brent Joh
 nson\, who will serve as interlocutor for this night of conversation and l
 ive acoustic music.\nThe night will begin with a moderated conversation be
 tween the two old friends\, with time for questions and comments from atte
 ndees. After that comes a short reading from I QUIT EVERYTHING\, followed 
 by a solo acoustic performance of songs from SOMETHING TO LOOK FORWARD TO 
 and others from John’s back catalogue. Books and albums will be available 
 for sale and signing.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20231028T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20231028T193000
LOCATION:Morgenstern's Books\, 849 Auto Mall Rd\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:An Evening of Music and Conversation with John Strohm & Freda Love 
 Smith
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/an-evening-of-music-and-conversation-with-john-s
 trohm-freda-love-smith/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Former bandma
 tes and Bloomingtonians John Strohm and Freda Love Smith reunite for a spe
 cial event to celebrate both the release of John’s new solo album\, SOMETH
 ING TO LOOK FORWARD TO\, and Freda’s new memoir\, I QUIT EVERYTHING. John 
 and Freda will be joined by WFIU’s David Brent Johnson\, who will serve as
  interlocutor for this night of conversation and live acoustic music.<br /
 >\nThe night will begin with a moderated conversation between the two old 
 friends\, with time for questions and comments from attendees. After that 
 comes a short reading from I QUIT EVERYTHING\, followed by a solo acoustic
  performance of songs from SOMETHING TO LOOK FORWARD TO and others from Jo
 hn’s back catalogue. Books and albums will be available for sale and signi
 ng.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
