BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-114700@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015805Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Kiwi journalist/filmmaker David Farrier is known for engaging w
 ith the stranger side of life in films like his stranger-than-fiction comp
 etitive tickling exposé Tickled\, drawing bizarre subjects into the light 
 through his affable but penetrating investigations. But Farrier may have m
 et his match with the subject of his latest doc\, a harrowing odyssey into
  the dark heart of a uniquely toxic individual. Things start innocently en
 ough in 2016\, as Farrier begins investigating a man who’s been clamping t
 he tires of cars parked outside an Auckland antiques shop\, then demanding
  hundreds of dollars to let them go. It turns out that the man behind the 
 clamping — the evocatively named Michael Organ — isn’t just a one-trick sc
 ammer. Organ has a colorful history of compulsive lying\, false identities
 \, and vindictive lawsuits stretching back decades. Farrier manages to est
 ablish a prickly\, ambiguous rapport with his elusive subject\, but the de
 eper he gets\, the more it seems that Organ’s intentions aren’t just eccen
 tric — they’re downright sinister.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20231103T183000
LOCATION:IU Fine Arts Theater (upstairs)\, 1201 East 7th Street\, on the no
 rth side of Showalter Fountain\, next to the IU Auditorium
RDATE;TZID=America/New_York:20231110T183000
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Ryder Film Series: MR ORGAN
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/ryder-film-series-mr-organ/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Kiwi journali
 st/filmmaker David Farrier is known for engaging with the stranger side of
  life in films like his stranger-than-fiction competitive tickling exposé 
 <em>Tickled\,</em> drawing bizarre subjects into the light through his aff
 able but penetrating investigations. But Farrier may have met his match wi
 th the subject of his latest doc\, a harrowing odyssey into the dark heart
  of a uniquely toxic individual. Things start innocently enough in 2016\, 
 as Farrier begins investigating a man who’s been clamping the tires of car
 s parked outside an Auckland antiques shop\, then demanding hundreds of do
 llars to let them go. It turns out that the man behind the clamping — the 
 evocatively named Michael Organ — isn’t just a one-trick scammer. Organ ha
 s a colorful history of compulsive lying\, false identities\, and vindicti
 ve lawsuits stretching back decades. Farrier manages to establish a prickl
 y\, ambiguous rapport with his elusive subject\, but the deeper he gets\, 
 the more it seems that Organ’s intentions aren’t just eccentric — they’re 
 downright sinister.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-114295@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015805Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:Josh Hamel\; josh.hamel@cambridge.org
DESCRIPTION:Professor Aurelian Craiutu will be discussing the recently publ
 ished book: “Why Not Moderation?: Letters to Young Radicals.” The book cha
 llenges the conventional image of political moderation as searching for a 
 fuzzy center between two extremes\, taking the form of a passionate modera
 te addressing two young radicals – one to the political left and one to th
 e political right.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20231103T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20231103T193000
LOCATION:Morgenstern Books at 849 S. Auto Mall Rd. Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Why Not Moderation?: Letters to Young Radicals
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/why-not-moderation-letters-to-young-radicals/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Professor Aur
 elian Craiutu will be discussing the recently published book: “Why Not Mod
 eration?: Letters to Young Radicals.” The book challenges the conventional
  image of political moderation as searching for a fuzzy center between two
  extremes\, taking the form of a passionate moderate addressing two young 
 radicals – one to the political left and one to the political right.</p>\n
 </BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
