BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-118357@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015805Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:The Palestine Solidarity Committee at Indiana University is hos
 ting world-renowned human rights lawyer\, Sarah Leah Whitson\, to talk abo
 ut how US support for Israel affects the rights of the Palestinian people.
  Whitson served as the head of the Middle East and North Africa division o
 f Human Rights Watch from 2004-2020 and currently serves as the executive 
 director of Democracy For the Arab World Now.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20240229T183000
LOCATION:Mauer Law School room 123\, 211 S Indiana Ave\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Palestine\, Human Rights and US Support For Israel
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/palestine-human-rights-and-us-support-for-israel
 /
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>The Palestine
  Solidarity Committee at Indiana University is hosting world-renowned huma
 n rights lawyer\, Sarah Leah Whitson\, to talk about how US support for Is
 rael affects the rights of the Palestinian people. Whitson served as the h
 ead of the Middle East and North Africa division of Human Rights Watch fro
 m 2004-2020 and currently serves as the executive director of Democracy Fo
 r the Arab World Now.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-118854@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015805Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:In 1977\, NASA struggled to recruit scientists\, engineers\, an
 d astronauts for their new Space Shuttle Program. That’s when Nichelle Nic
 hols\, Star Trek‘s Lt. Uhura\, challenged NASA by asking the question: “Wh
 ere are my people?” Nichols then embarked on a four-month campaign to recr
 uit the first Black\, Latino\, and Asian men and women to fly in space. [9
 6 min\; documentary\; English]\nA panel discussion will follow the screeni
 ng. Moderated by Dr. Sima Setayeshgar (IU Physics)\, the panel will includ
 e Dr. Erin Flowers\, Assistant Director of STEM Education at Princeton\; B
 rooke Kimsey-Miller\, doctoral candidate in Astronomy at IU\; Marvin Jones
 \, doctoral candidate in Astrophysics at IU\; and Anika Goel\, doctoral ca
 ndidate in Astronomy at IU.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20240229T190000
LOCATION:IU Cinema\, 1213 East Seventh St. (on the North side of the IU Aud
 itorium)\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Film: WOMAN IN MOTION
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/film-woman-in-motion/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>In 1977\, NAS
 A struggled to recruit scientists\, engineers\, and astronauts for their n
 ew Space Shuttle Program. That’s when Nichelle Nichols\, <i>Star Trek</i>‘
 s Lt. Uhura\, challenged NASA by asking the question: “Where are my people
 ?” Nichols then embarked on a four-month campaign to recruit the first Bla
 ck\, Latino\, and Asian men and women to fly in space. [96 min\; documenta
 ry\; English]</p>\n<p><i><strong>A panel discussion will follow the screen
 ing. Moderated by Dr. Sima Setayeshgar (IU Physics)\, the panel will inclu
 de Dr. Erin Flowers\, Assistant Director of STEM Education at Princeton\; 
 Brooke Kimsey-Miller\, doctoral candidate in Astronomy at IU\; Marvin Jone
 s\, doctoral candidate in Astrophysics at IU\; and Anika Goel\, doctoral c
 andidate in Astronomy at IU.</strong></i></p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
