UID:ai1ec-120655@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015805Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:If you navigate the hills and valleys of the western half of Mo
 nroe County\, you will pass many stands of dry stack stone walls. Wall sto
 nes have been repurposed near where walls once marked a property line or o
 ther notable location. Join Susan Snider Salmon as we look at the stories 
 of what is known and examine what remains to be rediscovered.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20240425T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20240425T193000
LOCATION:Monroe County History Center\, 202 E 6th St\, Bloomington\, IN 474
 08
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:No Rolling Stones: a Landscape Perspective of the Dry Stack Stone W
 alls of Monroe County
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/no-rolling-stones-a-landscape-perspective-of-the
 -dry-stack-stone-walls-of-monroe-county/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>If you naviga
 te the hills and valleys of the western half of Monroe County\, you will p
 ass many stands of dry stack stone walls. Wall stones have been repurposed
  near where walls once marked a property line or other notable location. J
 oin Susan Snider Salmon as we look at the stories of what is known and exa
 mine what remains to be rediscovered.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
