BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-121882@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015805Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:In this year’s annual Rosemary Miller Lecture\, Duncan Campbell
  remembers Nancy Hiller and her deep commitment to historic preservation\,
  the written word\, fine woodworking\, and personal ethics. A stalwart mem
 ber of the Bloomington community\, Nancy’s advocacy\, good humor\, and per
 sonal touch were felt by everyone she met\; and her writing\, furniture\, 
 and model of ethical citizenship reached well beyond Bloomington. Few have
  done as much for preservation\, or spread its word as far\, but it was he
 r readiness to so elegantly share her life’s work and intimate self-examin
 ation in her writing that truly set her apart.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20240524T180000
LOCATION:Council Chambers at City Hall\, 401 North Morton St.\, Room 115\, 
 Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Lecture: “Remembering Nancy Hiller: Preservationist\, Writer\, Wood
 worker\, Ethicist” by Duncan Campbell
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/talk-remembering-nancy-hiller-preservationist-wr
 iter-woodworker-ethicist-by-duncan-campbell/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>In this year’
 s annual Rosemary Miller Lecture\, Duncan Campbell remembers Nancy Hiller 
 and her deep commitment to historic preservation\, the written word\, fine
  woodworking\, and personal ethics. A stalwart member of the Bloomington c
 ommunity\, Nancy’s advocacy\, good humor\, and personal touch were felt by
  everyone she met\; and her writing\, furniture\, and model of ethical cit
 izenship reached well beyond Bloomington. Few have done as much for preser
 vation\, or spread its word as far\, but it was her readiness to so elegan
 tly share her life’s work and intimate self-examination in her writing tha
 t truly set her apart.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
