BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-121930@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015805Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:For the past thirty-five years Mike Van Vooren has been carving
  limestone.\nHe is also an avid amateur musician who plays guitar\, bass\,
  saxophone\, and drums\nand compares his approach to sculpting limestone t
 o that of an improvising jazz\nmusician. This show represents some of his 
 limestone carving from a musical\nperspective.\nMike has produced visual a
 rt in many media including drawing\, pastel\, oil\,\nacrylic\, digital pho
 tography\, and limestone. His work is represented in collections\nworld-wi
 de.\nUpon graduating with a degree in English Literature in 1972\, he\nimm
 ediately began an intensive and ongoing self-instruction in visual art and
 \nart history. He moved to Bloomington in 1974 where he was a founding\nme
 mber of the Artist’s Co-op of 25 community artists\, graduate students\nan
 d instructors at Indiana University.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20240607T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20240607T200000
LOCATION:By Hand Gallery\, 101 W Kirkwood Suite 109 inside Fountain Square 
 Mall\, Bloomington IN
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Gallery Walk Opening Reception for ROCK MUSIC. Limestone sculptures
  and reliefs by Mike Van Vooren.
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/gallery-walk-opening-reception-for-rock-music-li
 mestone-sculptures-and-reliefs-by-mike-van-vooren/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>For the past 
 thirty-five years Mike Van Vooren has been carving limestone.<br />\nHe is
  also an avid amateur musician who plays guitar\, bass\, saxophone\, and d
 rums<br />\nand compares his approach to sculpting limestone to that of an
  improvising jazz<br />\nmusician. This show represents some of his limest
 one carving from a musical<br />\nperspective.<br />\nMike has produced vi
 sual art in many media including drawing\, pastel\, oil\,<br />\nacrylic\,
  digital photography\, and limestone. His work is represented in collectio
 ns<br />\nworld-wide.<br />\nUpon graduating with a degree in English Lite
 rature in 1972\, he<br />\nimmediately began an intensive and ongoing self
 -instruction in visual art and<br />\nart history. He moved to Bloomington
  in 1974 where he was a founding<br />\nmember of the Artist’s Co-op of 25
  community artists\, graduate students<br />\nand instructors at Indiana U
 niversity.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
