BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-125527@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Christina Lockhart creates hand-carved block prints with inspir
 ation from her love of nature.  The blocks are carved and then hand-printe
 d\, which means there will be slight differences in every print\, making e
 ach one unique.\nSarah Nichols is an Indiana-born artist who chooses to fo
 cus on natural materials in her sewing\, weaving\, quilting\, and dyeing p
 ractices. She also dabbles in pottery and printmaking\, claiming that arti
 sts need hobbies too. Sarah’s work is heavily influenced by her family\, h
 er memories\, and those small things that exist everywhere in the world th
 at are tiny wonders to behold\, but are often gone before they’re noticed.
 \nSarabeth Noggle (Brown County): In committing to a composition\, pattern
 s emerge and change or disappear as I chew my thoughts about who I am and 
 why. All sorts of realizations and misunderstandings filter in as I work\,
  the goal is movement and to be now\, a quiet therapy\, the same work as t
 urning the compost.\nShiree Woodard: My current artwork is an exploration 
 of thoughtscapes- a synthesis of subjects I enjoy reading about such as ph
 ilosophy\, neuroscience\, and the nature of being. My images have fragment
 s of the familiar while presenting some imaginative possibilities. I invit
 e you to wonder alongside of me and make your own connections while contem
 plating new pathways.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20240906T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20240906T200000
LOCATION:By Hand Gallery\, 101 W Kirkwood Suite 109\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Opening Reception: “Ink & Paper” Prints by Christina Lockhart\, Sar
 a Nichols\, Sarabeth Noggle and Shiree Woodard
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/opening-reception-ink-paper-prints-by-christina-
 lockhart-sara-nichols-sarabeth-noggle-and-shiree-woodard/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p style='font-w
 eight: 400'>Christina Lockhart<strong> </strong>creates hand-carved block 
 prints with inspiration from her love of nature.  The blocks are carved an
 d then hand-printed\, which means there will be slight differences in ever
 y print\, making each one unique.</p>\n<p style='font-weight: 400'>Sarah N
 ichols is an Indiana-born artist who chooses to focus on natural materials
  in her sewing\, weaving\, quilting\, and dyeing practices. She also dabbl
 es in pottery and printmaking\, claiming that artists need hobbies too. Sa
 rah’s work is heavily influenced by her family\, her memories\, and those 
 small things that exist everywhere in the world that are tiny wonders to b
 ehold\, but are often gone before they’re noticed.</p>\n<p style='font-wei
 ght: 400'>Sarabeth Noggle (Brown County): In committing to a composition\,
  patterns emerge and change or disappear as I chew my thoughts about who I
  am and why. All sorts of realizations and misunderstandings filter in as 
 I work\, the goal is movement and to be now\, a quiet therapy\, the same w
 ork as turning the compost.</p>\n<p style='font-weight: 400'>Shiree Woodar
 d: My current artwork is an exploration of thoughtscapes- a synthesis of s
 ubjects I enjoy reading about such as philosophy\, neuroscience\, and the 
 nature of being. My images have fragments of the familiar while presenting
  some imaginative possibilities. I invite you to wonder alongside of me an
 d make your own connections while contemplating new pathways.</p>\n</BODY>
 </HTML>
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-125850@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Clarissa Bonet uses urban spaces as a dynamic backdrop for the 
 subtle\, often overlooked dramas of daily life in the city. Bonet studies 
 the shape and behavior of sunlight and shadow\, and then carefully constru
 cts street scenes that are suspended in time\, briefly illuminating the ps
 ychological space that runs under the daily routines.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20240906T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20240906T190000
LOCATION:Pictura Gallery\, 202 South Rogers St.\, Bloomington
