BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-126215@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:“OSOGBO: Art & Heritage” is a retrospective of Nigerian contemp
 orary art from the world-renowned Osogbo School of Art.\nThe Osogbo School
  of Art was spearheaded by two European artists\, Ulli Beier and Susanne W
 enger\, in the early 1960s as a cultural response to the colonialism and a
 ppropriation so blatantly apparent in the European modernism of the 20th c
 entury. Many of Modernism’s most recognizable artists created works direct
 ly based on African art ideas with no citation or credit given to their in
 fluencers. In an effort to highlight the strength\, beauty\, and originali
 ty of African art\, Beier and Wenger\, along with renowned Nigerian playwr
 ight\, Duro Ladipo\, started the Mbari Mbayo Art Club\, which directly led
  to the founding of the Osogbo School of Art.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20240913T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20240913T200000
LOCATION:Rosemary P. Miller Gallery\, Waldron Arts Center\, 122 S Walnut St
 \, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Opening Reception for “OSOGBO: Art and Heritage Exhibition”
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/opening-reception-for-osogbo-art-and-heritage-ex
 hibition/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p style='text-a
 lign: left'>“OSOGBO: Art & Heritage” is a retrospective of Nigerian contem
 porary art from the world-renowned Osogbo School of Art.</p>\n<p>The Osogb
 o School of Art was spearheaded by two European artists\, Ulli Beier and S
 usanne Wenger\, in the early 1960s as a cultural response to the coloniali
 sm and appropriation so blatantly apparent in the European modernism of th
 e 20th century. Many of Modernism’s most recognizable artists created work
 s directly based on African art ideas with no citation or credit given to 
 their influencers. In an effort to highlight the strength\, beauty\, and o
 riginality of African art\, Beier and Wenger\, along with renowned Nigeria
 n playwright\, Duro Ladipo\, started the Mbari Mbayo Art Club\, which dire
 ctly led to the founding of the Osogbo School of Art.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
