BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-126477@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:Elliot Josephine Leila Reichert\, Curator of Contemporary Art\; ell
 reich@indiana.edu\; https://events.iu.edu/artmuseum/event/a-conversation-w
 ith-samuel-levi-jones-and-daniel-lui
DESCRIPTION:Join for a conversation between artist Samuel Levi Jones and Pr
 ofessor Daniel Luis Martinez about the artist’s studio practice\, focusing
  on the museum’s recent acquisition Poplar Trees and their collaborative p
 roject.\nSamuel Levi Jones trained as a photographer and multidisciplinary
  artist. His work is informed by historical source material and early mode
 s of representation in documentary practice.\nDaniel Luis Martinez is an a
 rchitectural designer and educator. His research and creative work investi
 gate the transformation of undervalued sites through a synthesis of public
  art and public space design.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20240914T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20240914T170000
LOCATION:Sidney and Lois Eskenazi Museum of Art\, 1133 E 7th St\, Bloomingt
 on (Martin Commons Room)
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:A Conversation with Samuel Levi Jones and Daniel Luis Martinez
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/a-conversation-with-samuel-levi-jones-and-daniel
 -luis-martinez/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Join for a co
 nversation between artist Samuel Levi Jones and Professor Daniel Luis Mart
 inez about the artist’s studio practice\, focusing on the museum’s recent 
 acquisition Poplar Trees and their collaborative project.</p>\n<p><strong>
 Samuel Levi Jones</strong> trained as a photographer and multidisciplinary
  artist. His work is informed by historical source material and early mode
 s of representation in documentary practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Daniel Luis M
 artinez</strong> is an architectural designer and educator. His research a
 nd creative work investigate the transformation of undervalued sites throu
 gh a synthesis of public art and public space design.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
