BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-126709@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:The Outliers’ Last Chance Show Down completes the successful ex
 hibition of four artists from Bloomington’s fringes. Using acrylic on wood
 \, limestone and cast-iron sculptures\, and color work on paper and mixed 
 media\, four artists who have been in conversation\, culture\, and politic
 s for years explore the meaning of being within and beyond the main. This 
 exhibit presents work informed by and informing their wide-ranging discuss
 ions during that time.\nJeff Hartenfeld’s acrylic paintings on wood explor
 e issues of resilience\, conflict\, and home\, drawing on his 45 years of 
 farmwork in Greene County\, IN. Dave Diegel’s ink on paper and multimedia 
 constructions offer a concise window into this “seldom seen” but compellin
 g artist’s vision. Ariana Berman\, formerly the artist in residence at the
  Sculpture Trails of Solsberry\, Indiana\, and now based in Lexington\, KY
 \, presents organically-inspired\, “otherworldly” ironwork sculptures that
  evoke themes of impermanence and resilience in the face of illness. And B
 ert Gilbert’s iron\, limestone\, and mixed media sculptures\, poured\, car
 ved\, and fabricated in the Indiana Uplands\, explore the language of his 
 recycled materials through images and forms that invite dialog.\nTogether\
 , these artists present a thought-provoking and visually stunning collecti
 on that invites engagement with the many communities of creators among us.
  Other viewings by appointment.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20240927T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20240927T200000
LOCATION:I Fell Gallery\, 415 W 4th St\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:“Outliers” Show Down
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/outliers-show-down/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p><strong>The O
 utliers’ Last Chance Show Down</strong> completes the successful exhibitio
 n of four artists from Bloomington’s fringes. Using acrylic on wood\, lime
 stone and cast-iron sculptures\, and color work on paper and mixed media\,
  four artists who have been in conversation\, culture\, and politics for y
 ears explore the meaning of being within and beyond the main. This exhibit
  presents work informed by and informing their wide-ranging discussions du
 ring that time.</p>\n<p><strong>Jeff Hartenfeld</strong>’s acrylic paintin
 gs on wood explore issues of resilience\, conflict\, and home\, drawing on
  his 45 years of farmwork in Greene County\, IN. Dave Diegel’s ink on pape
 r and multimedia constructions offer a concise window into this “seldom se
 en” but compelling artist’s vision. Ariana Berman\, formerly the artist in
  residence at the Sculpture Trails of Solsberry\, Indiana\, and now based 
 in Lexington\, KY\, presents organically-inspired\, “otherworldly” ironwor
 k sculptures that evoke themes of impermanence and resilience in the face 
 of illness. And Bert Gilbert’s iron\, limestone\, and mixed media sculptur
 es\, poured\, carved\, and fabricated in the Indiana Uplands\, explore the
  language of his recycled materials through images and forms that invite d
 ialog.</p>\n<p>Together\, these artists present a thought-provoking and vi
 sually stunning collection that invites engagement with the many communiti
 es of creators among us. <em>Other viewings by appointment.</em></p>\n</BO
 DY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
