BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-127974@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:Marcia Veldman\, Green Drinks Bloomington\; 812.325.2446\; marciave
 ldman@gmail.com\; www.greendrinks.org
DESCRIPTION:A conversation about the contributions and global impact of Ind
 igenous and local knowledge\, values\, and practices for nature and the pl
 anet\nEduardo S. Brondizio (Distinguished Professor of Anthropology\, Indi
 ana University) will provide an overview of an exhibit that recently opene
 d at the IU Museum of Archaeology and Anthropology. The current accelerati
 ng global environmental crisis has heightened awareness of the knowledge\,
  values\, and practices of Indigenous Peoples and Local Communities. Indig
 enous Peoples’ contributions to both nature and society are wide-ranging a
 nd have been increasingly recognized in both scientific and Indigenous-led
  research. These contributions include various ways of identifying and man
 aging landscapes and waterscapes\; producing food and materials\, while ma
 intaining agrobiodiversity that supports a significant sector of the globa
 l population\; monitoring the health of local environments\; restoring eco
 systems or preventing their destruction\; and offering alternative ways of
  conceptualizing the relationship between nature and humanity. The exhibit
  explores these contributions.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20241023T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20241023T193000
LOCATION:Upland Brewing Wood Shop\, 354 W 11th St\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Green Drinks Bloomington presents: Prof Eduardo Brondizio\, “A conv
 ersation about the contributions and global impact of Indigenous and local
  knowledge\, values\, and practices for nature and the planet”
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/green-drinks-bloomington-presents-prof-eduardo-b
 rondizio-a-conversation-about-the-contributions-and-global-impact-of-indig
 enous-and-local-knowledge-values-and-practices-for-nature-and-the-planet/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>A conversatio
 n about the contributions and global impact of Indigenous and local knowle
 dge\, values\, and practices for nature and the planet</p>\n<p>Eduardo S. 
 Brondizio (Distinguished Professor of Anthropology\, Indiana University) w
 ill provide an overview of an exhibit that recently opened at the IU Museu
 m of Archaeology and Anthropology. The current accelerating global environ
 mental crisis has heightened awareness of the knowledge\, values\, and pra
 ctices of Indigenous Peoples and Local Communities. Indigenous Peoples’ co
 ntributions to both nature and society are wide-ranging and have been incr
 easingly recognized in both scientific and Indigenous-led research. These 
 contributions include various ways of identifying and managing landscapes 
 and waterscapes\; producing food and materials\, while maintaining agrobio
 diversity that supports a significant sector of the global population\; mo
 nitoring the health of local environments\; restoring ecosystems or preven
 ting their destruction\; and offering alternative ways of conceptualizing 
 the relationship between nature and humanity. The exhibit explores these c
 ontributions.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
