BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-127187@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:https://monroehistory.org/history-book-club/
DESCRIPTION:Where History Buffs and Bookworms Connect\nDo you love to read 
 books? How about books about history? If so\, join the HiStory Book Club t
 o discuss the great stories of our past over a glass of coffee or tea. Boo
 ks about historical figures\, places and times throughout Monroe County\, 
 Indiana\, and the world!\nIt’s simple – follow the reading recommendations
  below and mark your calendars. Admission is free and you don’t even have 
 to complete the book to attend and participate in the discussion. Come mee
 t some new friends as we talk about old times.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20241026T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20241026T143000
LOCATION:Monroe County History Center\, 202 E 6th St\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:HiStory Book Club: “The Facemaker” by Lindsey Fitzharris (Europe)
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/history-book-club-2024-10-26/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p><strong>Where
  History Buffs and Bookworms Connect</strong><br />\nDo you love to read b
 ooks? How about books about history? If so\, join the <strong>HiStory Book
  Club</strong> to discuss the great stories of our past over a glass of co
 ffee or tea. Books about historical figures\, places and times throughout 
 Monroe County\, Indiana\, and the world!</p>\n<p>It’s simple – follow the 
 reading recommendations below and mark your calendars. Admission is free a
 nd you don’t even have to complete the book to attend and participate in t
 he discussion. Come meet some new friends as we talk about old times.</p>
 \n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-127998@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:Bloomington Restorations\, Inc.\; 812-336-0909\; https://www.facebo
 ok.com/HinkleGartonFarmstead
DESCRIPTION:Bloomington Restorations\, Inc. invites you to the October Muse
 um Open Day\, “A Fall Gathering” at the Hinkle-Garton Farmstead. Volunteer
 s will give indoor tours of the Farmhouse. Located in the Music Room Parlo
 r\, Music will be performed with sing-along invited. \nFeatured for sale i
 nside the main house will be the delightful cookbook\, “Call to Lunch\,” a
  collection of Daisy Hinkle-Garton’s recipes. Volunteer Janette Robertson\
 , quilter and seamstress\, will be offering some of her homespun creations
  for sale. She will be donating a\npercentage of the sales to the Farmstea
 d. \nThe book on BRI’s affordable housing program\, From Historic Ruins to
  Affordable Homes\, Saving Bloomington’s Vintage Cottages will be offered 
 for sale. In addition\, offered for sale will be maple syrup made at the F
 armstead\, as well as maple syrup products supplied by the Indiana Maple S
 yrup Association. \nThe Hinkle-Garton Farmstead is sending a call out for 
 Volunteers who might be interested in assisting with Farmstead tours one S
 aturday per month (usually the last Saturday of designated months) from 1-
 4 PM. Volunteer duties include introducing visitors to the Farmstead histo
 ry and act as docents/hosts for the afternoon. Our Open Days are conducted
  in a very easy-going atmosphere\, minimal training and commitment require
 d and always under supervision and guidance. All that would be required is
  an ability to pleasantly engage the public and a 1-1/2\nhour training. If
  interested please contact LeAnn Luce\, Hinkle-Garton Farmstead Open Days 
 and Events Coordinator at: lucebella50@gmail.com for more information.\nTh
 e Farmstead\, located at 2920 E. Tenth Street in Bloomington\, is listed o
 n the National Register of Historic Places and is owned and maintained by 
 Bloomington Restorations\, Inc.\, an organization committed to the preserv
 ation of historic properties in Monroe County. It is free and open to the 
 public on the last Saturday of most months.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20241026T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20241026T160000
LOCATION:2920 E Tenth St\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Open Day at the Hinkle-Garton Farmstead: “A Fall Gathering”
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/open-day-at-the-hinkle-garton-farmstead-a-fall-g
 athering/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p><strong>Bloom
 ington Restorations\, Inc.</strong> invites you to the October Museum Open
  Day\, “A Fall Gathering” at the Hinkle-Garton Farmstead. Volunteers will 
 give indoor tours of the Farmhouse. Located in the Music Room Parlor\, Mus
 ic will be performed with sing-along invited. </p>\n<p>Featured for sale i
 nside the main house will be the delightful cookbook\, “Call to Lunch\,” a
  collection of Daisy Hinkle-Garton’s recipes. Volunteer Janette Robertson\
 , quilter and seamstress\, will be offering some of her homespun creations
  for sale. She will be donating a<br />\npercentage of the sales to the Fa
 rmstead. </p>\n<p>The book on BRI’s affordable housing program\, From Hist
 oric Ruins to Affordable Homes\, Saving Bloomington’s Vintage Cottages wil
 l be offered for sale. In addition\, offered for sale will be maple syrup 
 made at the Farmstead\, as well as maple syrup products supplied by the In
 diana Maple Syrup Association. </p>\n<p>The Hinkle-Garton Farmstead is sen
 ding a call out for Volunteers who might be interested in assisting with F
 armstead tours one Saturday per month (usually the last Saturday of design
 ated months) from 1-4 PM. Volunteer duties include introducing visitors to
  the Farmstead history and act as docents/hosts for the afternoon. Our Ope
 n Days are conducted in a very easy-going atmosphere\, minimal training an
 d commitment required and always under supervision and guidance. All that 
 would be required is an ability to pleasantly engage the public and a 1-1/
 2<br />\nhour training. If interested please contact LeAnn Luce\, Hinkle-G
 arton Farmstead Open Days and Events Coordinator at: lucebella50@gmail.com
  for more information.</p>\n<p>The Farmstead\, located at 2920 E. Tenth St
 reet in Bloomington\, is listed on the National Register of Historic Place
 s and is owned and maintained by Bloomington Restorations\, Inc.\, an orga
 nization committed to the preservation of historic properties in Monroe Co
 unty. It is free and open to the public on the last Saturday of most month
 s.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
