BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-128021@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Directed by Carolann Buff\nJoin the Bloomington Chamber Singers
  in the first concert of their 54th season\, as Guest Conductor Carolann B
 uff leads the choir in a program of iconic works by English composers from
  the Renaissance and the Twentieth Century. Experience one of Benjamin Bri
 tten’s most popular works\, Rejoice in the Lamb (1943) on fragments of a l
 ate 18th-century poem written from an insane asylum\; William Byrd’s subli
 me Mass for Four Voices (1592-3)\, composed for clandestine Catholic celeb
 rations in recusant households\, and Ralph Vaughan Williams’ colorful Five
  English Folk Songs (1913)\, which sends you on your way with a rousing Wa
 ssail song! The concert will be presented both live and livestreamed.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20241102T193000
LOCATION:First Presbyterian Church\, 221 E 6th St\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Bloomington Chamber Singers – English Choral Masters
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/bloomington-chamber-singers-english-choral-maste
 rs/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Directed by C
 arolann Buff<br />\nJoin the Bloomington Chamber Singers in the first conc
 ert of their 54th season\, as Guest Conductor Carolann Buff leads the choi
 r in a program of iconic works by English composers from the Renaissance a
 nd the Twentieth Century. Experience one of Benjamin Britten’s most popula
 r works\, Rejoice in the Lamb (1943) on fragments of a late 18th-century p
 oem written from an insane asylum\; William Byrd’s sublime Mass for Four V
 oices (1592-3)\, composed for clandestine Catholic celebrations in recusan
 t households\, and Ralph Vaughan Williams’ colorful Five English Folk Song
 s (1913)\, which sends you on your way with a rousing Wassail song! The co
 ncert will be presented both live and livestreamed.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
