BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-131047@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:Molly Gleeson\; https://www.writersguildbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:Presenter	The Writers Guild at Bloomington\nLinda Schelbitzki Pi
 ckle came to her family’s Nebraska farm in 1945\, soon after her father re
 turned to Chicago from World War II. Her parents worked hard to establish 
 themselves and Linda was deeply shaped by her youth spent there. She sees 
 these years now as a time of great change in American agriculture.\nAnn Ro
 bins began writing her family stories after retiring to Bloomington in 200
 6. In 2019 she received the daily journals and ledgers of the family farm 
 from 1932 to 1991. Ann swims\, grows and makes things\, volunteers at the 
 downtown library\, but continues to be obsessed with remembering her grand
 mother.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250202T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20250202T173000
LOCATION:Juniper Art Gallery and Cafe\, 615 W Kirkwood Ave\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:First Sunday Prose Reading\, feat. writers Ann Robins / Linda Pickl
 e
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/first-sunday-prose-reading-feat-writers-ann-robi
 ns-linda-pickle/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Presenter	The 
 Writers Guild at Bloomington<br />\n<strong>Linda Schelbitzki Pickle</stro
 ng> came to her family’s Nebraska farm in 1945\, soon after her father ret
 urned to Chicago from World War II. Her parents worked hard to establish t
 hemselves and Linda was deeply shaped by her youth spent there. She sees t
 hese years now as a time of great change in American agriculture.</p>\n<p>
 <strong>Ann Robins</strong> began writing her family stories after retirin
 g to Bloomington in 2006. In 2019 she received the daily journals and ledg
 ers of the family farm from 1932 to 1991. Ann swims\, grows and makes thin
 gs\, volunteers at the downtown library\, but continues to be obsessed wit
 h remembering her grandmother.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
