BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-131880@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:A striking cinematic vision that weaves through time and space\
 , the film follows Mack\, a Black woman in Mississippi\,  as she experienc
 es the joy\, grief\, love\, and pain that defines the journey from childho
 od to adulthood. Expertly leveraging the sensation of touch in a way uncom
 mon—and incredibly difficult—in cinema\, Jackson creates an ethereal and d
 eeply felt world that is simultaneously unfamiliar and yet instantly recog
 nizable. Co-produced by Barry Jenkins (Moonlight\, If Beale Street Could T
 alk)\, All Dirt Roads Taste of Salt premiered to rave reviews at the 2023 
 Sundance Film Festival and was named one of the Top 10 Independent Films o
 f 2023 by the National Board of Review. [92 min\; drama\; English]\nThe fi
 lm will be preceded by a recorded talk on experimental film and memory wor
 k by Maya Cade\, founder\, Black Film Archive.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250211T190000
LOCATION:IU Cinema\, 1213 East Seventh St. (on the North side of the IU Aud
 itorium)\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:AfroFutures: Black Women and Cinematic Storytelling Series – ALL DI
 RT ROADS TASTE OF SALT
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/afrofutures-black-women-and-cinematic-storytelli
 ng-series-film-all-dirt-roads-taste-of-salt/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>A striking ci
 nematic vision that weaves through time and space\, the film follows Mack\
 , a Black woman in Mississippi\,  as she experiences the joy\, grief\, lov
 e\, and pain that defines the journey from childhood to adulthood. Expertl
 y leveraging the sensation of touch in a way uncommon—and incredibly diffi
 cult—in cinema\, Jackson creates an ethereal and deeply felt world that is
  simultaneously unfamiliar and yet instantly recognizable. Co-produced by 
 Barry Jenkins (Moonlight\, If Beale Street Could Talk)\, All Dirt Roads Ta
 ste of Salt premiered to rave reviews at the 2023 Sundance Film Festival a
 nd was named one of the Top 10 Independent Films of 2023 by the National B
 oard of Review. [92 min\; drama\; English]</p>\n<p>The film will be preced
 ed by a recorded talk on experimental film and memory work by Maya Cade\, 
 founder\, Black Film Archive.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-131313@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Concert with Rhys Jones and Nokosee Fields – Old Time songs and
  tunes performed by a top-notch duo.\nRhys Jones is a fiddler extraordinai
 re. He has won fiddle and band contests at The Appalachian String Band Fes
 tival (Clifftop)\, and is regarded as “one of the bright lights of the cur
 rent generation of traditional performers.” Having spent much of his child
 hood in southern West Virginia (where he learned from the older generation
  of musicians)\, Rhys is equally at home telling the stories of Appalachia
 n music as he is performing on fiddle\, banjo\, and guitar.\nNokosee Field
 s\, a member of the Osage\, Creek\, and Cherokee Nations\, is one of the m
 ost provocative and capable voices in the North American traditional music
  scene. A quest for balance shapes his work as he challenges the demands a
 nd vacuity of colonialist\, capitalist structures and systems with the ric
 hness of his experience and upbringing. Nokosee also performs with The Onl
 ies\, Hard Drive\, and The Nokosee Fields Trio
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250211T190000
LOCATION:Lotus Firebay\, 105 S. Rogers St.\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Rhys Jones and Nokosee Fields
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/rhys-jones-and-nokosee-fields/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p><strong>Conce
 rt with Rhys Jones and Nokosee Fields – Old Time songs and tunes performed
  by a top-notch duo.</strong></p>\n<p>Rhys Jones is a fiddler extraordinai
 re. He has won fiddle and band contests at The Appalachian String Band Fes
 tival (Clifftop)\, and is regarded as “one of the bright lights of the cur
 rent generation of traditional performers.” Having spent much of his child
 hood in southern West Virginia (where he learned from the older generation
  of musicians)\, Rhys is equally at home telling the stories of Appalachia
 n music as he is performing on fiddle\, banjo\, and guitar.</p>\n<p>Nokose
 e Fields\, a member of the Osage\, Creek\, and Cherokee Nations\, is one o
 f the most provocative and capable voices in the North American traditiona
 l music scene. A quest for balance shapes his work as he challenges the de
 mands and vacuity of colonialist\, capitalist structures and systems with 
 the richness of his experience and upbringing. Nokosee also performs with 
 The Onlies\, Hard Drive\, and The Nokosee Fields Trio</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
