BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-130141@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Film | DOOR TIME 6:30 pm\nThe Science on Screen® grant initiati
 ve brings science to cinemas nationwide.\nThe Coolidge Corner Theatre’s se
 ries has enhanced film and scientific literacy with this popular program\,
  which launched at the Coolidge in 2005 in partnership with the Alfred P. 
 Sloan Foundation and its pioneering nationwide film program. This series p
 uts together creative pairings of current\, classic\, cult\, and documenta
 ry films with lively introductions by notable figures from the world of sc
 ience\, technology\, and medicine.\nMusic for Mushrooms (2024) is a narrat
 ive documentary highlighting the personal journey of shamanistic musician 
 and filmmaker East Forest (Krishna-Trevor Oswalt) – a collaborator of the 
 late Ram Dass – whose bold experiments pair music with guided psychedelic 
 experiences to create spaces of healing for a fractured world. The film ca
 ptures East Forest as he discovers a practice of guided ceremonial concert
 s that offer participants an immersive environment to confront their pain 
 and fears\, fostering hope\, and moving them to re-engage with their own t
 rue north.\nWeaving together interviews and conversations with artists\, a
 cademics\, and cultural experts in the fields of psychedelics\, science\, 
 music and philosophy – voices like Ram Dass\, Xochitl Ashe\, Hanifo Nayo W
 ashington\, Spring Washam\, Dr. Robin Carhart-Harris\, Jon Hopkins\, Peter
  Broderick\, ANNA\, and more – the film explores first hand experiences\, 
 wisdom traditions\, and data-backed evidence of how these timeless tools i
 mpact the human psyche\, nervous system\, and more.\nPre-Film Speaker: Dr.
  Richard Feldman\, MD is a family physician\, author\, lecturer\, and form
 er Indiana State Department of Health Commissioner. He has written about p
 silocybin and depression Can Magic Mushrooms Treat Depression? It Seems So
 .
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250220T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20250220T211500
LOCATION:Buskirk-Chumley Theater\, 114 E Kirkwood Ave\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:BCT presents: Music for Mushrooms (2024)
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/bct-presents-music-for-mushrooms-2024/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p><strong>Film 
 | DOOR TIME 6:30 pm</strong><br />\nThe <strong>Science on Screen®</strong
 > grant initiative brings science to cinemas nationwide.<br />\nThe Coolid
 ge Corner Theatre’s series has enhanced film and scientific literacy with 
 this popular program\, which launched at the Coolidge in 2005 in partnersh
 ip with the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation and its pioneering nationwide film 
 program. This series puts together creative pairings of current\, classic\
 , cult\, and documentary films with lively introductions by notable figure
 s from the world of science\, technology\, and medicine.</p>\n<p><strong>M
 usic for Mushrooms (2024)</strong> is a narrative documentary highlighting
  the personal journey of shamanistic musician and filmmaker East Forest (K
 rishna-Trevor Oswalt) – a collaborator of the late Ram Dass – whose bold e
 xperiments pair music with guided psychedelic experiences to create spaces
  of healing for a fractured world. The film captures East Forest as he dis
 covers a practice of guided ceremonial concerts that offer participants an
  immersive environment to confront their pain and fears\, fostering hope\,
  and moving them to re-engage with their own true north.</p>\n<p>Weaving t
 ogether interviews and conversations with artists\, academics\, and cultur
 al experts in the fields of psychedelics\, science\, music and philosophy 
 – voices like Ram Dass\, Xochitl Ashe\, Hanifo Nayo Washington\, Spring Wa
 sham\, Dr. Robin Carhart-Harris\, Jon Hopkins\, Peter Broderick\, ANNA\, a
 nd more – the film explores first hand experiences\, wisdom traditions\, a
 nd data-backed evidence of how these timeless tools impact the human psych
 e\, nervous system\, and more.</p>\n<p>Pre-Film Speaker: Dr. Richard Feldm
 an\, MD is a family physician\, author\, lecturer\, and former Indiana Sta
 te Department of Health Commissioner. He has written about psilocybin and 
 depression Can Magic Mushrooms Treat Depression? It Seems So.</p>\n</BODY>
 </HTML>
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-131099@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:dir. Rene Clair\nSynopsis: Blissfully hilarious and romantic\, 
 I MARRIED A WITCH features great chemistry between Veronica Lake (SULLIVAN
 ’S TRAVELS) and Fredric March (THE BEST YEARS OF OUR LIVES). This fun romp
  from French master Rene Clair gives its seasoned leads a chance to break 
 away from stereotypically serious roles (intense leading man and film noir
  vamp\, respectively) and prove their comedic prowess.\nLake stars as an a
 ncient Salem witch once burned at the stake\, who returns to haunt descend
 ants of the Puritans who let her smolder — namely aspiring politician Marc
 h.  Lake concocts a love potion\, but things get complicated when said pot
 ion works on Lake instead and she falls for March!\n*In partnership with T
 he Orbit Room\, The American Genre Film Archive and Vulture Video\, our Ci
 cada Underground series comes from the ethos that while as imperfect as th
 e people who made them\, genre films deserved to be screened to both enric
 h our lives and have a good time doing it!
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250220T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20250220T201500
LOCATION:Orbit Room\, downstairs at 107 N College Ave\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Cicada Cinema presents: I Married A Witch (1942)
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/cicada-cinema-presents-i-married-a-witch-1942/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>dir. Rene Cla
 ir<br />\nSynopsis: Blissfully hilarious and romantic\, I MARRIED A WITCH 
 features great chemistry between Veronica Lake (SULLIVAN’S TRAVELS) and Fr
 edric March (THE BEST YEARS OF OUR LIVES). This fun romp from French maste
 r Rene Clair gives its seasoned leads a chance to break away from stereoty
 pically serious roles (intense leading man and film noir vamp\, respective
 ly) and prove their comedic prowess.</p>\n<p>Lake stars as an ancient Sale
 m witch once burned at the stake\, who returns to haunt descendants of the
  Puritans who let her smolder — namely aspiring politician March.  Lake co
 ncocts a love potion\, but things get complicated when said potion works o
 n Lake instead and she falls for March!</p>\n<p>*In partnership with <stro
 ng>The Orbit Room</strong>\, <strong>The American Genre Film Archive</stro
 ng> and <strong>Vulture Video</strong>\, our <strong>Cicada Underground</s
 trong> series comes from the ethos that while as imperfect as the people w
 ho made them\, genre films deserved to be screened to both enrich our live
 s and have a good time doing it!</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
