BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-134007@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Unpacking Immigration (dir. Harleen Kaur Bal | 2023): Unpacking
  Immigration explores the lives of immigrant meatpackers whose unseen and 
 undervalued work bridges the crucial missing steps in “the farm to table” 
 concept in food production. This short film\, written and directed by Harl
 een Kaur Bal\, shares the story of a longtime Punjabi Sikh meatpacker in C
 alifornia’s Central Valley\, tracing his migration journey\, the human tol
 l of meatpacking work\, and the fraught notions of home and belonging for 
 working-class immigrants and their families in the “land of opportunity.” 
 Youth activism in the Jakara Movement will also be highlighted. [12 min\; 
 English]\nKai Hali’a (Sea of Memory) (dir. Angelique Kalani Axelrode | 202
 3): In the abstract realm of memory\, a diasporic Kanaka struggles to conn
 ect with their family and lover. By engaging with their moʻokūʻauhau (gene
 alogy) and calling on their kūpuna (ancestors) and ke kai (ocean)\, they a
 re able to cope with buried trauma and come back to themselves. Seeing mem
 ory as an intricate ʻupena (net) of both intangible and tangible threads o
 f reality\, intertwined with visceral feelings that intimately connect us 
 with our kūpuna (ancestors) and the ʻāina (land)\, the art of remembering 
 brings us back to our core. [9 min\; English and Hawaiian with open captio
 ns]\nWouldn’t Make It Any Other Way (dir. Hao Zhou | 2024): Having built a
  colorful queer life in an American prairie town\, an aspiring costume des
 igner visits their island homeland of Guam to make costumes for a children
 ’s theatre while reconnecting with distanced parents. [20 min\; English wi
 th open captions]\nPreserving Taste (dir. Gelareh Kiazand | 2023): Chef Ha
 nif Sadr\, born in Tehran\, Iran\, hopes to open his first Iranian regiona
 l restaurant in San Francisco. Through this journey\, he moves between Ira
 n and the US and explores the depth of a unique forgotten Persian cuisine.
  [21 min\; English]\nLeading (dir. Grace Lee | 2024): Leading follows two 
 budding activists: Audre (10)\, who fights against Asian hate by creating 
 a teach-in presentation at her school\; and Kali (11)\, who belongs to a R
 adical Monarchs troop\, where she learns about and helps organize a march 
 for Black Lives Matter. [27 min\; English]
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250411T190000
LOCATION:IU Cinema\, 1213 East Seventh St. (on the North side of the IU Aud
 itorium)\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:AAPI (Asian American Pacific Islander) Movement Short Film Festival
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/aapi-asian-american-pacific-islander-movement-sh
 ort-film-festival/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Unpacking Imm
 igration (dir. Harleen Kaur Bal | 2023): Unpacking Immigration explores th
 e lives of immigrant meatpackers whose unseen and undervalued work bridges
  the crucial missing steps in “the farm to table” concept in food producti
 on. This short film\, written and directed by Harleen Kaur Bal\, shares th
 e story of a longtime Punjabi Sikh meatpacker in California’s Central Vall
 ey\, tracing his migration journey\, the human toll of meatpacking work\, 
 and the fraught notions of home and belonging for working-class immigrants
  and their families in the “land of opportunity.” Youth activism in the Ja
 kara Movement will also be highlighted. [12 min\; English]</p>\n<p>Kai Hal
 i’a (Sea of Memory) (dir. Angelique Kalani Axelrode | 2023): In the abstra
 ct realm of memory\, a diasporic Kanaka struggles to connect with their fa
 mily and lover. By engaging with their moʻokūʻauhau (genealogy) and callin
 g on their kūpuna (ancestors) and ke kai (ocean)\, they are able to cope w
 ith buried trauma and come back to themselves. Seeing memory as an intrica
 te ʻupena (net) of both intangible and tangible threads of reality\, inter
 twined with visceral feelings that intimately connect us with our kūpuna (
 ancestors) and the ʻāina (land)\, the art of remembering brings us back to
  our core. [9 min\; English and Hawaiian with open captions]</p>\n<p>Would
 n’t Make It Any Other Way (dir. Hao Zhou | 2024): Having built a colorful 
 queer life in an American prairie town\, an aspiring costume designer visi
 ts their island homeland of Guam to make costumes for a children’s theatre
  while reconnecting with distanced parents. [20 min\; English with open ca
 ptions]</p>\n<p>Preserving Taste (dir. Gelareh Kiazand | 2023): Chef Hanif
  Sadr\, born in Tehran\, Iran\, hopes to open his first Iranian regional r
 estaurant in San Francisco. Through this journey\, he moves between Iran a
 nd the US and explores the depth of a unique forgotten Persian cuisine. [2
 1 min\; English]</p>\n<p>Leading (dir. Grace Lee | 2024): Leading follows 
 two budding activists: Audre (10)\, who fights against Asian hate by creat
 ing a teach-in presentation at her school\; and Kali (11)\, who belongs to
  a Radical Monarchs troop\, where she learns about and helps organize a ma
 rch for Black Lives Matter. [27 min\; English]</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
