BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-134126@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Sam Blasucci is best known as one half of Mapache\, a Southern 
 California roots-rock duo just as instantly recognizable for their elegant
 \, intertwined guitar parts as they are for their devoted\, Nudie-Suit wea
 ring fanbase. But when Blasucci was writing the songs that would become hi
 s debut solo record\, Off My Stars\, he found himself less focused on the 
 guitar and more gravitated toward a different instrument: piano.\nThe moth
 er of Clay Finch\, his Mapache bandmate\, was getting rid of one\, and so 
 Blasucci took the piano\, carefully transporting it to his home in Ojai\, 
 California\, with the help of a few strong friends\, including Farmer Dave
  Scher of Beachwood Sparks (and a Mapache collaborator). “Farmer Dave wasn
 ’t even wearing shoes\,” Blasucci remembers\, laughing. Once the piano was
  safely in there\, he became deeply attached\, playing on it multiple hour
 s a day: “It’s changed the way I think about music\, having all the keys l
 aid out in front of me\,” he explains. “Having that sort of changed everyt
 hing.”
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250414T200000
LOCATION:The Bishop Bar\, 123 South Walnut St.\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Sam Blasucci with Julia Zivic  (18+)
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/sam-blasucci-with-julia-zivic-18/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Sam Blasucci 
 is best known as one half of Mapache\, a Southern California roots-rock du
 o just as instantly recognizable for their elegant\, intertwined guitar pa
 rts as they are for their devoted\, Nudie-Suit wearing fanbase. But when B
 lasucci was writing the songs that would become his debut solo record\, Of
 f My Stars\, he found himself less focused on the guitar and more gravitat
 ed toward a different instrument: piano.</p>\n<p>The mother of Clay Finch\
 , his Mapache bandmate\, was getting rid of one\, and so Blasucci took the
  piano\, carefully transporting it to his home in Ojai\, California\, with
  the help of a few strong friends\, including Farmer Dave Scher of Beachwo
 od Sparks (and a Mapache collaborator). “Farmer Dave wasn’t even wearing s
 hoes\,” Blasucci remembers\, laughing. Once the piano was safely in there\
 , he became deeply attached\, playing on it multiple hours a day: “It’s ch
 anged the way I think about music\, having all the keys laid out in front 
 of me\,” he explains. “Having that sort of changed everything.”</p>\n</BOD
 Y></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
