BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-134149@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:RaMell Ross | 2024 | USA | PG-13 | DCP\nClosed captioning\nElwo
 od Curtis’s college dream shatters alongside a two-lane Florida highway. B
 earing the brunt of an innocent misstep\, he’s sentenced to the netherworl
 d of Nickel Academy\, a brutal reformatory sunk deep in the Jim Crow South
 . He encounters another ward\, the seen-it-all Turner. The two Black teens
  strike up an alliance: Turner dispensing fundamental tips for survival\, 
 Elwood clinging to his optimistic worldview. Backdropped by the burgeoning
  Civil Rights Movement\, Elwood and Turner’s existence appear worlds away 
 from Rev. Martin Luther King’s burnished oratory. Despite Nickel’s brutali
 ty\, Elwood strives to hold onto his humanity\, awakening a new vision for
  Turner. Based on the Pulitzer Prize-winning novel by Colson Whitehead. [1
 40 min\; drama\; English]
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250416T190000
LOCATION:IU Cinema\, 1213 East Seventh St. (on the North side of the IU Aud
 itorium)\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:New Americas Cinema Series: NICKEL BOYS
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/new-americas-cinema-series-nickel-boys/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>RaMell Ross |
  2024 | USA | PG-13 | DCP<br />\nClosed captioning<br />\nElwood Curtis’s 
 college dream shatters alongside a two-lane Florida highway. Bearing the b
 runt of an innocent misstep\, he’s sentenced to the netherworld of Nickel 
 Academy\, a brutal reformatory sunk deep in the Jim Crow South. He encount
 ers another ward\, the seen-it-all Turner. The two Black teens strike up a
 n alliance: Turner dispensing fundamental tips for survival\, Elwood cling
 ing to his optimistic worldview. Backdropped by the burgeoning Civil Right
 s Movement\, Elwood and Turner’s existence appear worlds away from Rev. Ma
 rtin Luther King’s burnished oratory. Despite Nickel’s brutality\, Elwood 
 strives to hold onto his humanity\, awakening a new vision for Turner. Bas
 ed on the Pulitzer Prize-winning novel by Colson Whitehead. [140 min\; dra
 ma\; English]</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-134156@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Monthly Folklore Talk\nJoe Johnson\nJoe Zavaan Johnson (he/they
 ) is a multi-instrumentalist\, arts educator\, Black music researcher\,\na
 nd native of Ohio. He is currently am Ethnomusicology Ph.D. Candidate at I
 ndiana University-Bloomington\, where his dissertation looks at the Black 
 banjo renaissance through the lenses of\nBlack studies\, human geography\,
  folklore\, and ethnomusicology. Recently\, Johnson was a\nfeatured artist
  at the Berkeley Old Time Music Convention\, the DeFord Bailey Legacy Fest
 ival\,\nand the Fort Worth African American Roots Music Festival. His ment
 ors include Jake Blount and Brad Leftwich.\nIsaiah Green\nIsaiah Green wil
 l delve into the broader\, often overlooked instances of witch trials\, sp
 anning from the 1500s in Italy to the present-day witch hunts in Tennessee
 . In this exploration\, he will connect the figure of the “witch”\; to the
  theories of monsters and monstrosity\, examining how societies across tim
 e and cultures use narratives of magic to justify and perpetuate gendered 
 and racial violence.\nNia I’man Smith\nNia I’man Smith is an interdiscipli
 nary Black music scholar and Ph.D. candidate in theDepartment of Folklore 
 and Ethnomusicology at IU Bloomington. Her research interests include acts
  and practices of ancestor veneration within Black music\, record collecti
 ng as an archival practice\, and Black-owned record stores as sites of cul
 tural preservation.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250416T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20250416T210000
LOCATION:Orbit Room\, 107 North College Ave.\, Suite 001 (downstairs)\, Blo
 omington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Presentations on Folklore and Ethnomusicology
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/presentations-on-folklore-and-ethnomusicology/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Monthly Folkl
 ore Talk</p>\n<p>Joe Johnson<br />\nJoe Zavaan Johnson (he/they) is a mult
 i-instrumentalist\, arts educator\, Black music researcher\,<br />\nand na
 tive of Ohio. He is currently am Ethnomusicology Ph.D. Candidate at Indian
 a University-Bloomington\, where his dissertation looks at the Black banjo
  renaissance through the lenses of<br />\nBlack studies\, human geography\
 , folklore\, and ethnomusicology. Recently\, Johnson was a<br />\nfeatured
  artist at the Berkeley Old Time Music Convention\, the DeFord Bailey Lega
 cy Festival\,<br />\nand the Fort Worth African American Roots Music Festi
 val. His mentors include Jake Blount and Brad Leftwich.</p>\n<p>Isaiah Gre
 en<br />\nIsaiah Green will delve into the broader\, often overlooked inst
 ances of witch trials\, spanning from the 1500s in Italy to the present-da
 y witch hunts in Tennessee. In this exploration\, he will connect the figu
 re of the “witch”\; to the theories of monsters and monstrosity\, examinin
 g how societies across time and cultures use narratives of magic to justif
 y and perpetuate gendered and racial violence.</p>\n<p>Nia I’man Smith<br 
 />\nNia I’man Smith is an interdisciplinary Black music scholar and Ph.D. 
 candidate in theDepartment of Folklore and Ethnomusicology at IU Bloomingt
 on. Her research interests include acts and practices of ancestor venerati
 on within Black music\, record collecting as an archival practice\, and Bl
 ack-owned record stores as sites of cultural preservation.</p>\n</BODY></H
 TML>
END:VEVENT
