CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:IU Graduating Studio Art Majors Present “The Artful Exit”\, an 
 art exhibition showcasing and celebrating knowledge gained\, on view at FA
 R.\nThe Artists:\nJulette Albert\, Ciover Childers\, Levi Croquart\, Emily
  Fahrnow\, Cate Garber\, Fin Howard\, Abby Knobeioch\, Elzabeth Means\, An
 a Serrano\, Margaret Thuesen\, Aalyail Tillmon\, Josh Toto\, Emmanuel Ufua
 h\, Ella Wolfinbarger\, Olvia Wright\nThe work on view is from the Studio 
 Art BA Seniors from the Eskenazi School of Art\, Architecture\, and Design
 . Students have spent the semester working on independent\, self-guided pr
 ojects that draw on their breadth of experience here at IU and in the Eske
 nazi School. Therefore\, the works include a dynamic range of media\, proc
 esses\, and ideas
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250502T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20250502T200000
LOCATION:FAR Center for Contemporary Arts\, 202 South Rogers St.\, Blooming
 ton
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:First Friday Gallery Walk Reception – The Artful Exit: BA Capstone 
 Exhibition
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/first-friday-gallery-walk-reception-the-artful-e
 xit-ba-capstone-exhibition/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>IU Graduating
  Studio Art Majors Present “The Artful Exit”\, an art exhibition showcasin
 g and celebrating knowledge gained\, on view at FAR.<br />\nThe Artists:<b
 r />\nJulette Albert\, Ciover Childers\, Levi Croquart\, Emily Fahrnow\, C
 ate Garber\, Fin Howard\, Abby Knobeioch\, Elzabeth Means\, Ana Serrano\, 
 Margaret Thuesen\, Aalyail Tillmon\, Josh Toto\, Emmanuel Ufuah\, Ella Wol
 finbarger\, Olvia Wright</p>\n<p>The work on view is from the Studio Art B
 A Seniors from the Eskenazi School of Art\, Architecture\, and Design. Stu
 dents have spent the semester working on independent\, self-guided project
 s that draw on their breadth of experience here at IU and in the Eskenazi 
 School. Therefore\, the works include a dynamic range of media\, processes
 \, and ideas</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-132895@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:An exploration of contrasts\, this exhibition brings together t
 he distinct artistic visions of Nicholas Graham Hall and Catherine Conlin.
  “Minimalism & Maximalism: A Comparative Exhibition” invites viewers to en
 gage in a visual dialogue between two seemingly opposing aesthetics.\nNich
 olas Graham Hall’s work explores minimalist concepts\, focusing on the ess
 ential elements of form\, color\, and texture. His pieces often employ res
 traint and simplicity\, inviting contemplation and a deeper connection wit
 h the core components of the artwork.\nIn contrast\, Catherine Conlin embr
 aces maximalism\, creating richly layered and detailed pieces that overwhe
 lm the senses. Her work is characterized by an abundance of imagery\, vibr
 ant colors\, and a celebration of excess.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250502T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20250502T200000
LOCATION:Backspace Gallery\, inside Bonne Fête\, 112 West 6th Street\, on t
 he square in Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:First Friday: Minimalism & Maximalism: A Comparative Exhibition
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/first-friday-minimalism-maximalism-a-comparative
 -exhibition/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>An exploratio
 n of contrasts\, this exhibition brings together the distinct artistic vis
 ions of Nicholas Graham Hall and Catherine Conlin. “Minimalism & Maximalis
 m: A Comparative Exhibition” invites viewers to engage in a visual dialogu
 e between two seemingly opposing aesthetics.</p>\n<p>Nicholas Graham Hall’
 s work explores minimalist concepts\, focusing on the essential elements o
 f form\, color\, and texture. His pieces often employ restraint and simpli
 city\, inviting contemplation and a deeper connection with the core compon
 ents of the artwork.</p>\n<p>In contrast\, Catherine Conlin embraces maxim
 alism\, creating richly layered and detailed pieces that overwhelm the sen
 ses. Her work is characterized by an abundance of imagery\, vibrant colors
 \, and a celebration of excess.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
