BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-136458@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:A far cry from the cool\, calculated distance and reserved post
 ure that is all-too-familiar to the indie-rock sphere\, Florry\, the Phill
 y-bred septet and songwriting vehicle of bandleader Francie Medosch\, are 
 marking their territory as a band resolving to do something very different
 : they are having a really good time out there.\nCutting her teeth in the 
 Philadelphia DIY scene starting in 2019 as a student at Temple University\
 , the early days of Florry found Medosch at the end of her teenage years r
 eleasing a slew of singles and EP’s in a familiar idiom of lo-fi bedroom r
 ecordings tinged with country melancholy. A lot has changed since then. Mo
 st importantly\, perhaps\, the project snowballed into a barn-burning seve
 n piece rock band in the proceeding years\; and without sacrificing any of
  the emotional immediacy that’s come to define Medosch’s brashly earnest\,
  bleeding-heart lyrical style\, you’re unlikely to find her lingering as m
 uch on the melancholy these days. Or\, as Medosch plainly puts it in regar
 ds to Sounds Like… \, the band’s forthcoming LP:
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250611T210000
LOCATION:The Bishop Bar\, 123 South Walnut St.\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Florry with Lily Seabird
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/florry-with-lily-seabird/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>A far cry fro
 m the cool\, calculated distance and reserved posture that is all-too-fami
 liar to the indie-rock sphere\, Florry\, the Philly-bred septet and songwr
 iting vehicle of bandleader Francie Medosch\, are marking their territory 
 as a band resolving to do something very different: they are having a real
 ly good time out there.</p>\n<p>Cutting her teeth in the Philadelphia DIY 
 scene starting in 2019 as a student at Temple University\, the early days 
 of Florry found Medosch at the end of her teenage years releasing a slew o
 f singles and EP’s in a familiar idiom of lo-fi bedroom recordings tinged 
 with country melancholy. A lot has changed since then. Most importantly\, 
 perhaps\, the project snowballed into a barn-burning seven piece rock band
  in the proceeding years\; and without sacrificing any of the emotional im
 mediacy that’s come to define Medosch’s brashly earnest\, bleeding-heart l
 yrical style\, you’re unlikely to find her lingering as much on the melanc
 holy these days. Or\, as Medosch plainly puts it in regards to Sounds Like
 … \, the band’s forthcoming LP:</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
