BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-138995@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:The City’s Assistant Director of Sustainability\, Shawn Miya\, 
 and Sustainability Program Coordinator\, Jolie Perry\, will highlight the 
 grants and rebates available to residents\, small businesses\, and nonprof
 its to reduce greenhouse gas emissions and increase resilience to climate 
 change. From solar panels\, batteries\, heat pumps\, and insulation\, to t
 ree plantings\, neighborhood gardens\, cool roofs\, and more\, the City pr
 ovides resources for the community to make these projects happen. Miya and
  Perry will also share news about potential incoming sources of federal gr
 ant dollars for community-wide energy efficiency and solar installations. 
 Listeners will find out how to apply for funding and what community input 
 the City needs for upcoming projects.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250827T180000
LOCATION:Upland Brewing Wood Shop\, 354 West 11th Street\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Climate Action in Bloomington: Together We Make a Difference
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/climate-action-in-bloomington-together-we-make-a
 -difference/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p><span id='m_2
 598782625655836700gmail-docs-internal-guid-30900576-7fff-5201-c0fa-db887c2
 66b8e'><strong>The City’s Assistant Director of Sustainability\, Shawn Miy
 a\, and Sustainability Program Coordinator\, Jolie Perry\, will highlight 
 the grants and rebates available to residents\, small businesses\, and non
 profits to reduce greenhouse gas emissions and increase resilience to clim
 ate change. From solar panels\, batteries\, heat pumps\, and insulation\, 
 to tree plantings\, neighborhood gardens\, cool roofs\, and more\, the Cit
 y provides resources for the community to make these projects happen.</str
 ong> Miya and Perry will also share news about potential incoming sources 
 of federal grant dollars for community-wide energy efficiency and solar in
 stallations. Listeners will find out how to apply for funding and what com
 munity input the City needs for upcoming projects.</span></p>\n</BODY></HT
 ML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-139365@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:**Cerridwen Kennedy** will share a collection of her own contem
 porary poetry\, centered on growing up in a neo-pagan household and her re
 lationship with spirituality as an adult.\nCerridwen is a poet and aspirin
 g author. She spent the majority of her childhood in libraries\, reading j
 ust about anything she could get her hands on. As an adult\, not much has 
 changed. She spends a lot of time between stacks of books\, exploring new 
 worlds\, and learning from other perspectives. Writing and telling stories
  is something she holds near and dear to her heart.\n**Jennifer Lynn Chris
 tie** will read a retelling of the French fairy tale\, “The White Cat” wri
 tten by Marjorie Sandor. Following her reading\, Jennifer will share her p
 erspective on why fairy tales are potent and fun vessels of modern storyte
 lling. Questions and conversation encouraged!\nJennifer’s short stories ha
 ve appeared in The Georgia Review\, PANK\, Atticus Review\, and elsewhere.
  She has written book reviews for the Denver Quarterly\, Entropy\, Newfoun
 d\, and Full Stop\, and for two years she served as the Assistant Small Pr
 ess Editor for Entropy.\nShe has recently completed her first novel and is
  seeking representation.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250827T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20250827T200000
LOCATION:Orbit Room\, 107 North College Ave.\, Suite 001 (downstairs)\, Blo
 omington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Folklore Night\, with presentations by Jennifer Lynn Christie\, and
  Cerridwen Kennedy
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/folklore-night-with-presentations-by-jennifer-ly
 nn-christie-and-cerridwen-kennedy/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>**Cerridwen K
 ennedy** will share a collection of her own contemporary poetry\, centered
  on growing up in a neo-pagan household and her relationship with spiritua
 lity as an adult.<br />\nCerridwen is a poet and aspiring author. She spen
 t the majority of her childhood in libraries\, reading just about anything
  she could get her hands on. As an adult\, not much has changed. She spend
 s a lot of time between stacks of books\, exploring new worlds\, and learn
 ing from other perspectives. Writing and telling stories is something she 
 holds near and dear to her heart.<br />\n**Jennifer Lynn Christie** will r
 ead a retelling of the French fairy tale\, “The White Cat” written by Marj
 orie Sandor. Following her reading\, Jennifer will share her perspective o
 n why fairy tales are potent and fun vessels of modern storytelling. Quest
 ions and conversation encouraged!<br />\nJennifer’s short stories have app
 eared in The Georgia Review\, PANK\, Atticus Review\, and elsewhere. She h
 as written book reviews for the Denver Quarterly\, Entropy\, Newfound\, an
 d Full Stop\, and for two years she served as the Assistant Small Press Ed
 itor for Entropy.<br />\nShe has recently completed her first novel and is
  seeking representation.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
