BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-140237@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Numerous reports from German-occupied Poland document a peculia
 r penchant of the Nazi perpetrators to torture Jews by forcing them to per
 form caricatured dances. These testimonies from World War II survivors and
  witnesses resonate with an older cultural phenomenon of caricaturizing si
 nging and dancing Jews.\nThe concept of “the Dancing Jew” has a long and c
 omplex history in Polish culture\, having originated in representations fo
 und in the traditional nativity plays (szopka) and gaining popularity thro
 ughout the nineteenth century in lowbrow theater. By the end of the centur
 y\, it also found its way into Polish- and Yiddish-language literary works
 .\nThe lecture draws on untapped archival sources and existing scholarly d
 iscourse to contextualize our understanding of the cultural phenomenon of 
 “the Dancing Jew\,” focusing on the case study of the ballet Karczma (The 
 Inn).
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250915T150000
LOCATION:IU Cinema\, 1213 East Seventh St. (on the North side of the IU Aud
 itorium)\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Distinguished Faculty Research Lecture: “The Dancing Jew” in Polish
  Culture: Between Phantasm and Practice
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/distinguished-faculty-research-lecture-the-danci
 ng-jew-in-polish-culture-between-phantasm-and-practice/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Numerous repo
 rts from German-occupied Poland document a peculiar penchant of the Nazi p
 erpetrators to torture Jews by forcing them to perform caricatured dances.
  These testimonies from World War II survivors and witnesses resonate with
  an older cultural phenomenon of caricaturizing singing and dancing Jews.<
 /p>\n<p>The concept of “the Dancing Jew” has a long and complex history in
  Polish culture\, having originated in representations found in the tradit
 ional nativity plays (szopka) and gaining popularity throughout the ninete
 enth century in lowbrow theater. By the end of the century\, it also found
  its way into Polish- and Yiddish-language literary works.</p>\n<p>The lec
 ture draws on untapped archival sources and existing scholarly discourse t
 o contextualize our understanding of the cultural phenomenon of “the Danci
 ng Jew\,” focusing on the case study of the ballet Karczma (The Inn).</p>
 \n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
