UID:ai1ec-140250@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Various bluegrass performers\, workshops\, and jams plus the Bl
 uegrass Hall of Fame Induction.\nFounded in 1974\, Uncle Pen Days began as
  a tribute to Bill Monroe’s maternal uncle\, James Pendleton Vandiver. “Un
 cle Pen” played the fiddle and Monroe later said that he was the “fellow t
 hat I learned to play from.” Bill Monroe’s parents had both died by the ti
 me he was 16\, and Monroe lived part of the time this his uncle\, where he
  would play mandolin while his uncle played fiddle at local square dances 
 and social events.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250919T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20250919T220000
LOCATION:Bill Monroe's Music Park & Campground\, 5163 Indiana Highway 135\,
  Bean Blossom\, IN
RDATE;TZID=America/New_York:20250919T100000
RDATE;TZID=America/New_York:20250920T100000
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Uncle Pen Fest – Bluegrass Festival and Hall of Fame Induction
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/uncle-pen-fest-bluegrass-festival-and-hall-of-fa
 me-induction/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Various blueg
 rass performers\, workshops\, and jams plus the Bluegrass Hall of Fame Ind
 uction.</p>\n<p>Founded in 1974\, Uncle Pen Days began as a tribute to Bil
 l Monroe’s maternal uncle\, James Pendleton Vandiver. “Uncle Pen” played t
 he fiddle and Monroe later said that he was the “fellow that I learned to 
 play from.” Bill Monroe’s parents had both died by the time he was 16\, an
 d Monroe lived part of the time this his uncle\, where he would play mando
 lin while his uncle played fiddle at local square dances and social events
 .</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
