BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-140270@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:IU Jacobs School of Music Musicology Department
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Sergio Ospina-Romero\, Assistant Professor of Music\, 
 Indiana University Jacobs School of Music\nThe circulation of sound record
 ings became a matter of everyday life in Latin America and the Caribbean e
 arly in the twentieth century. While the imperial operations of Victor\, C
 olumbia\, Edison\, Odeon\, and other businesses enhanced transnational net
 works for the global dissemination of their merchandise\, recorded sound a
 lso gained cultural currency because of their increasing relevance in all 
 kinds of social spaces. By digging into a variety of scenarios of music co
 nsumption and into the contents of sound recordings themselves\, this talk
  examines the uncontrolled circulation of talking machines and records in 
 Latin America and the Caribbean at the time\, showing how\, ultimately\, t
 he very tenets of everyday life became increasingly and inevitably entangl
 ed with those of recorded sound.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250919T124500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20250919T134500
LOCATION:Simon Music Center room SM344\, 200 S. Eagleson Ave\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Musicology Colloquium Series: The Everyday Life of Recorded Sound
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/musicology-colloquium-series-the-everyday-life-o
 f-recorded-sound/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Speaker: Serg
 io Ospina-Romero\, Assistant Professor of Music\, Indiana University Jacob
 s School of Music</p>\n<p>The circulation of sound recordings became a mat
 ter of everyday life in Latin America and the Caribbean early in the twent
 ieth century. While the imperial operations of Victor\, Columbia\, Edison\
 , Odeon\, and other businesses enhanced transnational networks for the glo
 bal dissemination of their merchandise\, recorded sound also gained cultur
 al currency because of their increasing relevance in all kinds of social s
 paces. By digging into a variety of scenarios of music consumption and int
 o the contents of sound recordings themselves\, this talk examines the unc
 ontrolled circulation of talking machines and records in Latin America and
  the Caribbean at the time\, showing how\, ultimately\, the very tenets of
  everyday life became increasingly and inevitably entangled with those of 
 recorded sound.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
