BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-140400@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015806Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:A Hispanic Heritage Month Film & Lecture Series.\nTaking place 
 in a Mayan village at the base of an active volcano\, “Ixcabul” centers on
  Maria and her family who live and work at a coffee plantation. The film o
 pens with an engagement ceremony between Maria and the plantation foreman\
 , Ignacio. Director Jayro Bustamante sought to challenge the reputation of
  French as a law status language in Guatamala by filming completely in Kaq
 chikel. \nThe screening will be preceded by IU PhD student K. Ermalinda Ga
 rcía. Their research centers the lives and organizing practices of women i
 n the L(G)BT community in Mexico City throughout the 20th century.\nThroug
 hout Hispanic Heritage Month—September 15 through October 15—every Saturda
 y evening will feature a powerful feature-length film\, a short lecture fr
 om a graduate scholar or recent Ph.D. graduate\, and an open discussion wh
 ere everyone is welcome to share their thoughts.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20250920T183000
LOCATION:Redbud Books\, 408 West Kirkwood Ave.\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Re-Encuentros Series:  “Ixcabul”
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/re-encuentros-series-ixcabul/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p><strong>A His
 panic Heritage Month Film & Lecture Series.</strong><br />\nTaking place i
 n a Mayan village at the base of an active volcano\, “Ixcabul” centers on 
 Maria and her family who live and work at a coffee plantation. The film op
 ens with an engagement ceremony between Maria and the plantation foreman\,
  Ignacio. Director Jayro Bustamante sought to challenge the reputation of 
 French as a law status language in Guatamala by filming completely in Kaqc
 hikel. </p>\n<p>The screening will be preceded by IU PhD student K. Ermali
 nda García. Their research centers the lives and organizing practices of w
 omen in the L(G)BT community in Mexico City throughout the 20th century.<b
 r />\n<strong>Throughout Hispanic Heritage Month—September 15 through Octo
 ber 15—every Saturday evening will feature a powerful feature-length film\
 , a short lecture from a graduate scholar or recent Ph.D. graduate\, and a
 n open discussion where everyone is welcome to share their thoughts.</stro
 ng> </p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
