BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-141167@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015807Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:How do you keep your loved ones close? During the 19th century\
 , Victorians considered hair to be a precious material and would create in
 tricately woven pieces of jewelry out of their loved one’s hair. The Wylie
  family is known to have collected loved one’s hair as sentimental objects
 . Come learn about this historic art form and make your own modern take! (
 NO REAL HAIR WILL BE REQUIRED)
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20251018T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20251018T140000
LOCATION:Wylie House Museum\, 307 E 2nd St\, Bloomington
RDATE;TZID=America/New_York:20251025T110000
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Saturday Crafternoon at the Wylie House
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/saturday-crafternoon-at-the-wylie-house-5/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><div>How do you 
 keep your loved ones close? During the 19th century\, Victorians considere
 d hair to be a precious material and would create intricately woven pieces
  of jewelry out of their loved one’s hair. The Wylie family is known to ha
 ve collected loved one’s hair as sentimental objects. Come learn about thi
 s historic art form and make your own modern take! (NO REAL HAIR WILL BE R
 EQUIRED)</div>\n<div></div>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
