BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-142116@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015807Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:In 1973\, U.S. businessman Ed Horman (Jack Lemmon) arrives in C
 hile to look for his son\, Charles (John Shea)\, a politically left-leanin
 g journalist who disappeared during a military coup. Charles’ wife\, Beth 
 (Sissy Spacek)\, has been looking for some time\, but her requests for hel
 p from the U.S. consulate have thus far produced few results. As they try 
 to figure out what really happened to Charles\, Ed realizes that the Ameri
 can officials may know more than they’re telling. [122 min\; thriller\, dr
 ama\; English\, Spanish\, and French with English subtitles]
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20251101T190000
LOCATION:IU Cinema\, 1213 East Seventh St. (on the North side of the IU Aud
 itorium)\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:McRobbie’s Choice Series: MISSING
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/mcrobbies-choice-series-missing/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>In 1973\, U.S
 . businessman Ed Horman (Jack Lemmon) arrives in Chile to look for his son
 \, Charles (John Shea)\, a politically left-leaning journalist who disappe
 ared during a military coup. Charles’ wife\, Beth (Sissy Spacek)\, has bee
 n looking for some time\, but her requests for help from the U.S. consulat
 e have thus far produced few results. As they try to figure out what reall
 y happened to Charles\, Ed realizes that the American officials may know m
 ore than they’re telling. [122 min\; thriller\, drama\; English\, Spanish\
 , and French with English subtitles]</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-142063@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015807Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:On this special night\, the enchanting Oak plant shop and cozy 
 Cafe Thistle become the stage for an extraordinary musical journey led by 
 Santa Morena\; Pat Otto joined by acclaimed musicians Dena El Saffar and T
 im Moore of Salaam\, along with the ever-grooving Steve Mascari on bass.\n
 Together\, this quartet conjures an eclectic and soulful mix—gypsy jazz\, 
 choro\, international folk\, and more—each tune a spell that stirs the hea
 rt and moves the spirit.\nRSVP is required—space is limited and magic awai
 ts.\nLet the music carry you across borders and time\, wrapped in the warm
 \, living energy of one of Bloomington’s most unique spaces.
