BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-142037@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015807Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:The Jacobs School of Music Opera and Ballet Theater presents “L
 a Bohème”. Based on Henri Murger’s 1851 novel Scenes de la vie de Bohème\,
  Puccini’s fourth opera is one of the world’s most popular and marked his 
 true coming of age as a composer\, and the end of his days as a starving a
 rtist. With themes so universal that it has successfully moved generations
 —from its polarizing 1896 premiere in Turin\, conducted by the 28-year-old
  Arturo Toscanini\, to inspiring the smash musical Rent on Broadway a cent
 ury later—La Bohème is part of popular culture.\nThis story of struggling 
 bohemian artists and their friends\, including Mimi and Rodolfo\, in Left 
 Bank Paris celebrates the joy\, excitement\, and carefree abandon of youth
 \, as they dare to live their lives and emotions to the fullest. But even 
 such passion cannot keep tragedy at bay.\n\nJoin us an hour before each pe
 rformance for the Opera Insights Lecture\, located in the North Lobby of t
 he Musical Arts Center.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20251114T193000
LOCATION:Musical Arts Center\, 101 North Eagleson Ave.\, Bloomington 
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:“La Bohème” by Giacomo Puccini
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/la-boheme-by-giacomo-puccini-3/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><div>The Jacobs 
 School of Music Opera and Ballet Theater presents “La Bohème”. Based on He
 nri Murger’s 1851 novel Scenes de la vie de Bohème\, Puccini’s fourth oper
 a is one of the world’s most popular and marked his true coming of age as 
 a composer\, and the end of his days as a starving artist. With themes so 
 universal that it has successfully moved generations—from its polarizing 1
 896 premiere in Turin\, conducted by the 28-year-old Arturo Toscanini\, to
  inspiring the smash musical Rent on Broadway a century later—La Bohème is
  part of popular culture.</div>\n<div>This story of struggling bohemian ar
 tists and their friends\, including Mimi and Rodolfo\, in Left Bank Paris 
 celebrates the joy\, excitement\, and carefree abandon of youth\, as they 
 dare to live their lives and emotions to the fullest. But even such passio
 n cannot keep tragedy at bay.</div>\n<div></div>\n<div>Join us an hour bef
 ore each performance for the Opera Insights Lecture\, located in the North
  Lobby of the Musical Arts Center.</div>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
