  Scenes de la vie de Bohème\, Puccini’s fourth opera is one of the world’s
  most popular and marked his true coming of age as a composer\, and the en
 d of his days as a starving artist. With themes so universal that it has s
 uccessfully moved generations—from its polarizing 1896 premiere in Turin\,
  conducted by the 28-year-old Arturo Toscanini\, to inspiring the smash mu
 sical Rent on Broadway a century later—La Bohème is part of popular cultur
 e.\nThis story of struggling bohemian artists and their friends\, includin
 g Mimi and Rodolfo\, in Left Bank Paris celebrates the joy\, excitement\, 
 and carefree abandon of youth\, as they dare to live their lives and emoti
 ons to the fullest. But even such passion cannot keep tragedy at bay.\n\nJ
