BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-142996@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015807Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:In early-1970s Framingham\, Massachusetts\, taciturn family man
  James (Josh O’Connor) makes the rash\, largely inscrutable decision to or
 chestrate a heist at the local art museum\, absconding with a selection of
  modern paintings—without much of a plan. This autumnal\, restrained\, and
  often quite funny anti-thriller from Kelly Reichardt (Showing Up\, NYFF60
 ) sets this low-key criminal enterprise against a Nixon-era backdrop of al
 ienation and political disillusionment. As always\, Reichardt’s impeccable
  craft is front and center: the film’s naturalism and remarkable period de
 tail creating a portrait of unerring authenticity and psychological myster
 y. Whether driven by social apathy or artistic passion\, James—effortlessl
 y played by O’Connor with hangdog elegance—registers as a compelling updat
 e of the ’70s American male loner archetype for another dispiriting\, dire
 ctionless time.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20251123T180000
LOCATION:FAR Center for Contemporary Arts\, 202 South Rogers St.\, Blooming
 ton
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Cicada Cinema: The Mastermind
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/cicada-cinema-the-mastermind/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>In early-1970
 s Framingham\, Massachusetts\, taciturn family man James (Josh O’Connor) m
 akes the rash\, largely inscrutable decision to orchestrate a heist at the
  local art museum\, absconding with a selection of modern paintings—withou
 t much of a plan. This autumnal\, restrained\, and often quite funny anti-
 thriller from Kelly Reichardt (Showing Up\, NYFF60) sets this low-key crim
 inal enterprise against a Nixon-era backdrop of alienation and political d
 isillusionment. As always\, Reichardt’s impeccable craft is front and cent
 er: the film’s naturalism and remarkable period detail creating a portrait
  of unerring authenticity and psychological mystery. Whether driven by soc
 ial apathy or artistic passion\, James—effortlessly played by O’Connor wit
 h hangdog elegance—registers as a compelling update of the ’70s American m
 ale loner archetype for another dispiriting\, directionless time. </p>\n</
 BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
