BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-143256@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015807Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Robert Reich\, the former U.S. Secretary of Labor\, might be fa
 mous for his public service\, best-selling books\, and viral social media 
 posts\, but he always considered teaching his true calling. Drawing on his
  lifetime in politics\, he uses his class\, “Wealth and Poverty\,” to offe
 r us all a deeper look at why inequalities of income and wealth have widen
 ed significantly since the late 1970s\, and why this poses dangerous risks
  to our society. Now\, after more than 40 years and an extraordinary 40\,0
 00 students\, Reich is preparing for his last class—confronting the impend
 ing finality\, and his own aging\, with increasing candor\, introspection\
 , and\, ultimately\, emotion. One thousand students fill the biggest lectu
 re hall on the UC Berkeley campus\, the last class to receive Reich’s wisd
 om and exhortations not to accept that the world has to stay the way it is
 . His belief in the next generation’s ability to take on the fight is insp
 iring. [71 min\; documentary\; English]
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20251203T190000
LOCATION:IU Cinema\, 1213 East Seventh St. (on the North side of the IU Aud
 itorium)\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:New Americas Cinema Series: THE LAST CLASS
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/new-americas-cinema-series-the-last-class/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Robert Reich\
 , the former U.S. Secretary of Labor\, might be famous for his public serv
 ice\, best-selling books\, and viral social media posts\, but he always co
 nsidered teaching his true calling. Drawing on his lifetime in politics\, 
 he uses his class\, “Wealth and Poverty\,” to offer us all a deeper look a
 t why inequalities of income and wealth have widened significantly since t
 he late 1970s\, and why this poses dangerous risks to our society. Now\, a
 fter more than 40 years and an extraordinary 40\,000 students\, Reich is p
 reparing for his last class—confronting the impending finality\, and his o
 wn aging\, with increasing candor\, introspection\, and\, ultimately\, emo
 tion. One thousand students fill the biggest lecture hall on the UC Berkel
 ey campus\, the last class to receive Reich’s wisdom and exhortations not 
 to accept that the world has to stay the way it is. His belief in the next
  generation’s ability to take on the fight is inspiring. [71 min\; documen
 tary\; English] </p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
