BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-144506@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015807Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Presented by Traveling University.\nOur well being depends far 
 more on the wealth we share than on our private finances and possessions. 
 This talk by Scott Russell Sanders\, Professor Emeritus of English at Indi
 ana University and Fellow of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences\, w
 ill celebrate the gifts of nature and culture that support and enrich our 
 lives. It will also show how these shared riches—atmosphere\, oceans\, bio
 diversity\, parks and public schools\, arts and science\, laws and languag
 e\, and many more—are under assault by those who wish to exploit them for 
 personal or corporate gain. By recognizing the common wealth that sustains
  us\, we may be moved to defend it. Questions\, reactions\, and insights w
 ill be welcomed.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260111T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260111T163000
LOCATION:Unitarian Universalist Church of Bloomington\, 2120 N. Fee Lane\, 
 Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:A Talk by Scott Russell Sanders: “Common Wealth: Living in a Shared
  World”
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/common-wealth-living-in-a-shared-world/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Presented by 
 Traveling University.<br />\nOur well being depends far more on the wealth
  we share than on our private finances and possessions. This talk by Scott
  Russell Sanders\, Professor Emeritus of English at Indiana University and
  Fellow of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences\, will celebrate the 
 gifts of nature and culture that support and enrich our lives. It will als
 o show how these shared riches—atmosphere\, oceans\, biodiversity\, parks 
 and public schools\, arts and science\, laws and language\, and many more—
 are under assault by those who wish to exploit them for personal or corpor
 ate gain. By recognizing the common wealth that sustains us\, we may be mo
 ved to defend it. Questions\, reactions\, and insights will be welcomed.</
 p>\n</BODY></HTML>
END:VEVENT
