BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-144649@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015807Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:This film was programmed in collaboration with Pictura Gallery’
 s current exhibit “I saw a tree bearing stones in the place of apples and 
 pears” by Emilia Martin\nSynopsis: Werner Herzog’s 32nd feature-length doc
 umentary and his second with volcanologist Clive Oppenheimer as co-directo
 r. Fireball is a remarkable journey across our planet and universe explori
 ng how meteorites\, shooting stars\, and deep impacts have awoken our wond
 er about other realms.\nOver billions of years\, extraterrestrial bombardm
 ents have reshaped the very nature of our planet\, causing species to peri
 sh or flourish — and perhaps even seeding the initial building blocks for 
 life on Earth. Today we can still hear the echo of these meteor impacts\, 
 ringing through culture\, mythology\, and scientific research. Fireball ta
 kes viewers into the mystery at the heart of these fallen stones\, explori
 ng their origins and their impacts on civilization.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260114T190000
LOCATION:505 West 4th St. (part of FAR Center)\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Cicada Cinema: Fireball: Visitors From Darker Worlds
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/cicada-cinema-fireball-visitors-from-darker-worl
 ds/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p><strong>This 
 film was programmed in collaboration with Pictura Gallery’s current exhibi
 t “I saw a tree bearing stones in the place of apples and pears” by Emilia
  Martin</strong><br />\nSynopsis: Werner Herzog’s 32nd feature-length docu
 mentary and his second with volcanologist Clive Oppenheimer as co-director
 . Fireball is a remarkable journey across our planet and universe explorin
 g how meteorites\, shooting stars\, and deep impacts have awoken our wonde
 r about other realms.</p>\n<p>Over billions of years\, extraterrestrial bo
 mbardments have reshaped the very nature of our planet\, causing species t
 o perish or flourish — and perhaps even seeding the initial building block
 s for life on Earth. Today we can still hear the echo of these meteor impa
 cts\, ringing through culture\, mythology\, and scientific research. Fireb
 all takes viewers into the mystery at the heart of these fallen stones\, e
 xploring their origins and their impacts on civilization.</p>\n</BODY></HT
 ML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
