BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-146415@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015807Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:Wait Wait… Don’t Tell Me! is National Public Radio’s Peabody Aw
 ard-winning comedy news quiz show. Host Peter Sagal leads a rotating panel
  of comedians\, writers\, listener contestants\, and celebrity guests thro
 ugh a rollicking review of the week’s news. Contestants vie for the most c
 oveted prize in all of public radio: a custom-recorded greeting by any of 
 our cast members for their voicemail.\nEvery week\, Wait\, Wait…. Don’t Te
 ll Me! records in front of a live audience\, then carefully edits out all 
 the raunchy\, inappropriate\, shocking\, and hilarious stuff so we can bro
 adcast something that doesn’t get NPR in trouble… well\, more trouble. The
  only way you can find out what our panelists and guests really think abou
 t the news is to come see for yourself. And please—don’t tell NPR. Also\, 
 we’re all much more attractive than we sound.
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260226T193000
LOCATION:IU Auditorium\, 1211 East 7th St.\, Bloomington
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Wait Wait… Don’t Tell Me!
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/wait-wait-dont-tell-me/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>Wait Wait… Do
 n’t Tell Me! is National Public Radio’s Peabody Award-winning comedy news 
 quiz show. Host Peter Sagal leads a rotating panel of comedians\, writers\
 , listener contestants\, and celebrity guests through a rollicking review 
 of the week’s news. Contestants vie for the most coveted prize in all of p
 ublic radio: a custom-recorded greeting by any of our cast members for the
 ir voicemail.</p>\n<p>Every week\, Wait\, Wait…. Don’t Tell Me! records in
  front of a live audience\, then carefully edits out all the raunchy\, ina
 ppropriate\, shocking\, and hilarious stuff so we can broadcast something 
 that doesn’t get NPR in trouble… well\, more trouble. The only way you can
  find out what our panelists and guests really think about the news is to 
 come see for yourself. And please—don’t tell NPR. Also\, we’re all much mo
 re attractive than we sound.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai1ec-144829@wfhb.org
DTSTAMP:20260331T015807Z
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:In Jesus and John Wayne\, Du Mez showed how white evangelicals 
 embraced a militant faith that sought power by any means. In her forthcomi
 ng book on white Christian femininity\, she examines how Christian women’s
  culture went hand in hand to create religious and political systems of au
 thority and control.\nAbout the series: The Department of History’s annual
  McNutt Lecture honors Paul V. McNutt\, who was dean of the Indiana Univer
 sity School of Law from 1925 to 1933\, then became Indiana’s governor and 
 later served as U.S. High Commissioner to the Philippines\, director of th
 e Federal Security Agency\, and chairman of the War Manpower Commission du
 ring World War II.\n 
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260226T193000
LOCATION:Indiana Memorial Union\, Tudor Room\, 900 East 7th Street\, Bloomi
 ngton
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:“From Jesus and John Wayne to Live Laugh Love: the Religious Roots 
 of our Current Political Moment” McNutt Lecture by Kristin Kobes Du Mez\, 
 Professor of History at Calvin University
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/from-jesus-and-john-wayne-to-live-laugh-love-the
 -religious-roots-of-our-current-political-moment-mcnutt-lecture-by-kristin
 -kobes-du-mez-professor-of-history-at-calvin-university/
X-COST-TYPE:free
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//E
 N'>\\n<HTML>\\n<HEAD>\\n<TITLE></TITLE>\\n</HEAD>\\n<BODY><p>In <em>Jesus 
 and John Wayne</em>\, Du Mez showed how white evangelicals embraced a mili
 tant faith that sought power by any means. In her forthcoming book on whit
 e Christian femininity\, she examines how Christian women’s culture went h
 and in hand to create religious and political systems of authority and con
 trol.</p>\n<p><strong>About the series</strong>: The Department of History
 ’s annual McNutt Lecture honors Paul V. McNutt\, who was dean of the India
 na University School of Law from 1925 to 1933\, then became Indiana’s gove
 rnor and later served as U.S. High Commissioner to the Philippines\, direc
 tor of the Federal Security Agency\, and chairman of the War Manpower Comm
 ission during World War II.</p>\n<p> </p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-INSTANT-EVENT:1
END:VEVENT
